IFL_DATA.register("cases", [{"id":"c-salam-wheat","kind":"textbook","title":"Wheat Salam with a third-party promise (Box 10.4)","chapter":10,"topicId":"t10.10","modeIds":["salam"],"scenario":"A farmer needs funds before harvest. On 1 January Bank B buys 5,000 tons of wheat from him for Rs.100 million paid immediately, delivery on 1 May. Company C promises to buy the wheat from B for Rs.115 million on 1 May and pays Rs.15 million as Hamish Jiddiyah.","facts":["Salam price Rs.100m paid in full on 1 January","Delivery 1 May","Promise by C: Rs.115m, HJ Rs.15m"],"questions":[{"prompt":"Why must B pay the full Rs.100 million on 1 January?","options":["To earn interest","Full prepayment is a condition of Salam","Because C paid HJ","It is optional"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Salam capital must be paid at the contract (Section 10.4.2)."},{"prompt":"If C refuses to buy on 1 May and B sells the wheat in the market for Rs.108 million, what may B recover from the HJ?","options":["Rs.15m","Rs.7m (actual loss)","Nothing","Rs.15m plus cost of funds"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Only actual loss (115 − 108 = 7) may be recovered from Hamish Jiddiyah; the rest is returned. (Practice extension — generated for learning.)"},{"prompt":"Could B sell the wheat to C before taking delivery by an ordinary sale?","options":["Yes","No — goods bought on Salam cannot be sold before possession; B may use a promise or Parallel Salam","Only if C is a bank","Only at cost"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Section 10.6."}],"analysis":"B earns Rs.15m gross by bearing delivery, quality and price risk until it sells. The promise gives B a buyer but does not itself sell the wheat before delivery.","takeaways":["Full prepayment","Promise + HJ protects against refusal","Actual loss only"],"source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.10","pages":[256,263]},"productIds":["salam-agri"],"origin":["f1"]},{"id":"c-ijarah-car","kind":"textbook","title":"Leased car destroyed (Box 11.3)","chapter":11,"topicId":"t11.6","modeIds":["ijarah"],"scenario":"A car leased under IMBT is destroyed without the lessee's negligence. The bank's outstanding investment plus prepaid expenses are Rs.370,000; the Takaful claim received is Rs.450,000; the client had paid a Rs.50,000 security deposit.","facts":["Outstanding investment + prepaid: Rs.370,000","Takaful claim: Rs.450,000","Security deposit: Rs.50,000"],"questions":[{"prompt":"What happens to the lease?","options":["Continues","Terminates; rent stops","Doubles","Converts to Murabaha"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Destruction of the identified asset without lessee fault ends the Ijarah (Section 11.5.1)."},{"prompt":"How much should the client receive per AAOIFI 8/8?","options":["Rs.50,000","Rs.80,000","Rs.130,000","Nothing"],"correctAnswer":2,"explanation":"Deposit 50,000 + Takaful surplus 80,000 (450,000 − 370,000) = Rs.130,000 (Box 11.3)."}],"analysis":"The lessor bears ownership risk, so the Takaful claim belongs to it; but since the client paid rent calculated to transfer ownership, AAOIFI 8/8 returns the excess to the client.","takeaways":["Lessor bears destruction risk","No rent after destruction","Surplus refunded (AAOIFI 8/8)"],"source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.6","pages":[298,305]},"productIds":["auto-ijarah"],"origin":["f1"]},{"id":"c-dm-house","kind":"textbook","title":"Housing Diminishing Musharakah (Box 12.5)","chapter":12,"topicId":"t12.9.2","modeIds":["diminishing-musharakah"],"scenario":"A client buys a house costing 1.0 million with 80% bank financing over 10 years. The bank's share (800,000) is divided into 120 monthly units; rent is 7% p.a. on the bank's outstanding investment.","facts":["Bank share 800,000; client 200,000","120 units of 6,666.67","Rent 7% p.a. on outstanding"],"questions":[{"prompt":"What is the month-1 payment?","options":["6,666.67","4,666.67","11,333.33","11,294.44"],"correctAnswer":2,"explanation":"6,666.67 unit + 800,000 × 7% ÷ 12 = 4,666.67 → 11,333.33."},{"prompt":"If the client delays, what happens to the rent?","options":["It stops","It does not decrease — rent continues on units the bank still owns","It doubles","It becomes interest"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Box 12.5 commentary."},{"prompt":"May the unit price be pre-agreed in this structure?","options":["No, never","Yes — it is DM in Shirkatulmilk","Only in trade","Only with a guarantee"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Section 12.8."}],"analysis":"Because the bank earns rent on its share, delay reduces its income less than in Murabaha; a pre-agreed price schedule is permitted because the partnership is in ownership rather than a trading business.","takeaways":["Rent falls as units are bought","Three separate contracts","Pre-agreed price only in Shirkatulmilk"],"source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.9.2","pages":[340,346]},"productIds":["home-dm"],"origin":["f1"]},{"id":"c-sitara","kind":"textbook","title":"Sitara Chemical's Musharakah TFCs (Box 12.3)","chapter":12,"topicId":"t12.7.3","modeIds":["musharakah"],"scenario":"Sitara Chemical issued 5-year Musharakah-based TFCs of Rs.360 million in 2002. Level I: on the first Rs.100 million of operating profit, 12% p.a. of outstanding principal (reduced proportionately if profit is lower). Level II: 2% per further Rs.100 million, one quarter to a Takaful reserve.","facts":["Rs.360m, 5 years","Level I: (OP/100m) × 12%","Level II: 2% per extra 100m; ¼ to Takaful reserve"],"questions":[{"prompt":"Operating profit is Rs.75 million. Level I rate?","options":["12%","9%","7.5%","2%"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"75/100 × 12% = 9%. (Practice figure applying Box 12.3's formula.)"},{"prompt":"If the company makes an operating loss, how is it absorbed?","options":["Company guarantees principal","First from the Takaful reserve, then against principal at redemption","By increasing profit rate","Not absorbed"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Box 12.3."}],"analysis":"Returns are genuinely linked to operating results and investors bear losses — a real PLS instrument. In practice returns were 15–24% p.a.","takeaways":["Profit linked to performance","Loss sharing via reserve then principal"],"source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.7.3","pages":[334,337]},"productIds":["musharakah-tfc","musharakah-sukuk"],"origin":["f1"]},{"id":"c-cotton","kind":"textbook","title":"Salam + Murabaha for cotton (Box 14.2)","chapter":14,"topicId":"t14.3.2","modeIds":["salam"],"scenario":"A bank buys cotton from growers on Salam. A textile mill promises to buy it at an agreed price. At harvest the bank appoints the mill as its agent to take delivery, then sells to the mill on Murabaha.","facts":["Salam with growers","Mill's promise","Mill as agent for delivery","Murabaha sale to mill"],"questions":[{"prompt":"Who benefits from price protection?","options":["Only the bank","Farmer (cash and price risk managed) and mill (hedge)","Nobody","Only the government"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Box 14.2."},{"prompt":"When may the bank sell to the mill?","options":["Before delivery by the growers","After the mill, as agent, has taken delivery on the bank's behalf","At the Salam contract date","Any time"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Goods must be in the bank's possession (via agent) before its Murabaha sale."}],"analysis":"Combining modes deploys funds longer, spreads exposure across two sectors and removes the inventory problem of pure Salam.","takeaways":["Combine modes, keep contracts separate","Agent possession precedes sale"],"source":{"chapter":14,"section":"14.3.2","pages":[360,362]},"productIds":[],"origin":["f1"]},{"id":"c-bill","kind":"textbook","title":"Musawamah alternative to bill discounting (Section 14.4.2)","chapter":14,"topicId":"t14.4.2","modeIds":["musawamah"],"scenario":"On 1 January an exporter wants cash against a US$100,000 bill due in 55 days. Spot is Rs.57.75/US$. The Islamic bank quotes Rs.57.60 and funds Rs.5,760,000 under an agency agreement for raw materials; the client then buys the stock from the bank for US$100,000 payable on the bill's due date.","facts":["Bill US$100,000, 55 days","Spot 57.75; bank rate 57.60","Funding Rs.5,760,000"],"questions":[{"prompt":"Why is ordinary bill discounting not allowed?","options":["It is too slow","It is sale of debt at a discount — Riba","It needs an L/C","It is Tawarruq"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Section 14.4.2."},{"prompt":"What turns this into a Shari’ah-compliant transaction?","options":["A lower rate","Real purchase of goods by the bank (via agent) and sale to the client for a deferred dollar price","A guarantee","Rollover"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Musawamah on goods owned by the bank."}],"analysis":"The bank earns through a sale of goods it owned, not by discounting a receivable.","takeaways":["No debt discounting","Real goods, real ownership"],"source":{"chapter":14,"section":"14.4.2","pages":[370,373]},"productIds":["export-bill-musawamah","post-shipment-qard"],"origin":["f1"]},{"id":"c-idb","kind":"textbook","title":"IDB Solidarity Trust Sukuk (2003)","chapter":15,"topicId":"t15.3.5.d","modeIds":["sukuk"],"scenario":"IDB issued US$400 million of trust certificates backed by a portfolio of Ijarah, Murabaha and Istisna‘a assets. Ijarah assets had to exceed 50%; if they fell below 25%, a dissolution event triggered IDB's purchase undertaking. IDB guaranteed obligors' scheduled payments and provided liquidity.","facts":["US$400m","Ijarah > 50% at all times","< 25% Ijarah → dissolution","Redemption at 100% of principal"],"questions":[{"prompt":"Why must Ijarah assets exceed 50%?","options":["For higher yield","So the Sukuk represent mainly tangible assets and can trade at market price","To satisfy LIBOR","For tax"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Mixed pool rule (Sections 15.3.5, 14.4.3)."},{"prompt":"What does IDB's guarantee cover?","options":["The certificates as such","Scheduled payments by obligors of the underlying assets","Nothing","Currency risk"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Section 15.3.5."}],"analysis":"A mixed pool converts illiquid receivables into tradable paper as long as tangible assets dominate; the guarantee structure raises the fixed-return concern discussed in Section 15.3.7.","takeaways":[">50% tangible","Guarantee of obligor payments","Fixed-return debate"],"source":{"chapter":15,"section":"15.3.5","pages":[406,407]},"productIds":["mixed-portfolio-sukuk"],"origin":["f1"]},{"id":"c-pak-sukuk","kind":"textbook","title":"Pakistan's sovereign Ijarah Sukuk (Box 15.6)","chapter":15,"topicId":"t15.4.cases","modeIds":["sukuk"],"scenario":"In January 2005 Pakistan International Sukuk Company bought M-2 motorway land from the National Highway Authority, issued US$600 million of trust certificates at six-month LIBOR + 220 bps, and leased the land to the government, which will repurchase it at maturity.","facts":["US$600m; orders US$1.2bn","Asset: M-2 motorway land","Rent: 6-month LIBOR + 220 bps","Rated B+"],"questions":[{"prompt":"What structure is this?","options":["Bai‘ al ‘Inah","Sale and lease-back Ijarah Sukuk","Mudarabah Sukuk","Salam Sukuk"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Box 15.6."},{"prompt":"Can these certificates trade at market price?","options":["No, only par","Yes — they represent ownership of the leased asset","Only to the government","Only after maturity"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Box 15.3: Ijarah Sukuk of existing assets trade at market price."}],"analysis":"A sovereign with durable assets can replace interest-based borrowing; Shari’ah scholars recommend time gaps and true sale to avoid resemblance to ‘Inah.","takeaways":["Government needs real assets","Rent benchmark as pricing tool"],"source":{"chapter":15,"section":"15.4","pages":[412,415]},"productIds":["ijarah-sukuk"],"origin":["f1"]},{"id":"c-takaful-fund","kind":"practice","title":"Takaful fund year-end (practice — generated for learning)","chapter":16,"topicId":"t16.4.1","modeIds":["takaful"],"scenario":"A Wakalah–Waqf Takaful fund receives Rs.20 million of contributions. The operator's fee is 25%. Claims and re-Takaful cost Rs.12 million. Investment profit on the fund is Rs.1 million, shared 40:60 between operator (Mudarib) and fund.","facts":["Contributions 20m","Fee 25%","Claims + re-Takaful 12m","Investment profit 1m, split 40:60"],"questions":[{"prompt":"Operator's Wakalah fee?","options":["Rs.2m","Rs.5m","Rs.8m","Rs.4m"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"25% × 20m = Rs.5m."},{"prompt":"Underwriting surplus before investment profit?","options":["Rs.8m","Rs.3m","Rs.15m","Rs.0"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"20 − 5 − 12 = Rs.3m, belonging to participants."},{"prompt":"Fund's share of investment profit?","options":["Rs.0.4m","Rs.0.6m","Rs.1m","Rs.0.25m"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"60% × 1m."}],"analysis":"The operator earns a fee and a Mudarib share but does not take the underwriting surplus — consistent with the preferred model in Section 16.4.1.","takeaways":["Fee + Mudarib share for operator","Surplus to participants"],"source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.4.1","pages":[423,426]},"productIds":["takaful"],"origin":["f1"]},{"id":"c-musharakah-loss","kind":"practice","title":"Trade Musharakah with a loss (practice — generated for learning)","chapter":12,"topicId":"t12.3.4","modeIds":["musharakah"],"scenario":"A bank (Rs.6m) and an importer (Rs.4m) finance a consignment on Musharakah. The importer manages and gets 50% of profit. The goods are damaged by a flood (no negligence) and sold at an overall loss of Rs.1m.","facts":["Capital 60:40","Profit ratio 50:50","Loss Rs.1m, no negligence"],"questions":[{"prompt":"Bank's share of the loss?","options":["Rs.0.5m","Rs.0.6m","Rs.1m","Nothing"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Loss follows capital: 60% × 1m."},{"prompt":"Can the bank enforce the importer's security for this loss?","options":["Yes","No — security covers only negligence, misconduct or breach","Only half","Only if agreed"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Section 12.3.5."}],"analysis":"PLS means the bank genuinely shares losses; collateral protects only against negligence.","takeaways":["Loss by capital","Collateral limited to negligence"],"source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.3.4","pages":[316,320]},"productIds":[],"origin":["f1"]},{"id":"c-mpo-agent","kind":"practice","title":"Murabaha documentation check (practice — generated for learning)","chapter":9,"topicId":"t9.8.3","modeIds":["murabaha"],"scenario":"A bank signs an agency agreement on 10 March. The client's supplier invoice is dated 5 March, and the client declares possession and offers to buy on 20 March after already using half the goods in production on 18 March.","facts":["Agency 10 March","Invoice 5 March","Goods partly consumed 18 March","Offer 20 March"],"questions":[{"prompt":"What is wrong with the invoice date?","options":["Nothing","It predates the agency — the client bought for himself, making resale to him ‘Inah-like","It is too late","Invoices are not needed"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Invoice must not be earlier than the agency (Box 9.1 and Section 9.8.3)."},{"prompt":"What about goods consumed before the offer?","options":["Fine","Murabaha cannot be executed on goods that no longer exist","Increase the price","Treat as Ijarah"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Goods must exist at offer and acceptance (Section 14.3.4)."}],"analysis":"Sequencing and existence of goods decide compliance; income from such transactions should go to charity per Shari’ah board rulings.","takeaways":["Agency before purchase","Goods must exist at sale"],"source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.8.3","pages":[225,229]},"productIds":["working-capital-murabaha"],"origin":["f1"]},{"id":"c-wapda","kind":"textbook","title":"WAPDA’s local-currency Ijarah Sukuk (Box 15.7)","chapter":15,"topicId":"t15.4.cases","modeIds":["sukuk"],"scenario":"WAPDA needed funds to expand power generation. An SPV, WAPDA First Sukuk Co (WFS), bought ten generation units at the Mangla Hydel Power Station from WAPDA and leased them back to WAPDA for seven years. The issue raised PKR 8,000 million. Rent is 6-month KIBOR + 35 bps, paid semi-annually. At maturity WAPDA buys the turbines back under a unilateral undertaking, and the government of Pakistan guarantees WAPDA’s payment obligation.","facts":["PKR 8,000m, privately placed floating-rate notes","Asset: ten Mangla turbines; 7-year lease-back","Rent: 6-m KIBOR + 35 bps, semi-annual","Government guarantee; eligible for banks’ SLR"],"questions":[{"prompt":"What do the Sukuk holders own?","options":["A loan to WAPDA","Through WFS, the turbines, with the related ownership risk","WAPDA shares","A claim on KIBOR"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Ijarah Sukuk must represent ownership of the leased assets, not only the right to rent (pp. 402, 414)."},{"prompt":"Rent is tied to KIBOR. Does that make the Sukuk interest-based?","options":["Yes","No — mainstream scholars allow an interest benchmark to price rent, though not ideal; the lessor still bears ownership risk","Only if KIBOR rises","Only for banks"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 401; Chapter 11 on benchmarks."},{"prompt":"How does WAPDA’s repurchase avoid being ‘Inah?","options":["It does not","It is a unilateral undertaking to buy after a seven-year lease, not a condition-linked immediate buy-back","Because the government guarantees it","Because the rent is floating"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Sale and lease-back is allowed if the sale is complete first; scholars recommend at least a year before repurchase (p. 397)."},{"prompt":"Why could Islamic banks hold these Sukuk for their statutory liquidity requirement?","options":["Because they pay interest","Because the government guaranteed WAPDA’s payment obligation","Because they are shares","Because they are Salam"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Box 15.7."}],"analysis":"A standard sovereign-type sale and lease-back. The Shari’ah questions are whether ownership genuinely passed to WFS and whether the contracts are independent. The book’s wider concern (Section 15.3.7) is that returns presented as fixed sit uneasily with the lessor’s ownership risks.","takeaways":["Ownership, not just rent, must pass","Benchmark is a pricing tool","Repurchase by separate undertaking","Guarantee made it SLR-eligible"],"source":{"chapter":15,"section":"15.4","pages":[412,415]},"productIds":["ijarah-sukuk"],"origin":["f1"]},{"id":"c-nakheel","kind":"textbook","title":"Nakheel’s US$3.52 billion Sukuk (Box 15.5)","chapter":15,"topicId":"t15.4.cases","modeIds":["sukuk"],"scenario":"Nakheel, the property arm of Dubai’s DP World group, launched a three-year Sukuk structured as a convertible sale and lease-back. The initial offer of US$2.5 billion was raised to US$3.52 billion, priced at LIBOR + 120 bps and listed on the Dubai International Financial Exchange. Barclays Capital and Dubai Islamic Bank were joint lead managers. Security included a mortgage on land, a pledge of shares in the operating company and a guarantee from Dubai World.","facts":["US$3.52bn (up from 2.5bn), 3 years, LIBOR + 120 bps","About 100 accounts: 38 % Middle East, 40 % Europe, 22 % rest of world","55 % banks, 35 % fixed-income and convertible funds","Mortgage, share pledge, Dubai World guarantee"],"questions":[{"prompt":"If LIBOR is 5.25 %, what periodic rate do holders receive?","options":["5.25 %","6.45 %","1.20 %","6.25 %"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"5.25 + 1.20 = 6.45 % (Box 15.5)."},{"prompt":"What does the investor profile suggest, according to the chapter?","options":["Sukuk appeal only to Gulf investors","Strong demand beyond the Muslim world — 40 % European","Sukuk are illiquid","Only individuals buy Sukuk"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 411."},{"prompt":"Which feature raises the Section 15.3.7 concern most directly?","options":["Listing on an exchange","A return presented as conclusively fixed, backed by a group guarantee","The use of a lead manager","The three-year tenor"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"The book questions pre-fixed returns and issuer-side guarantees (pp. 409–410)."}],"analysis":"The book presents Nakheel as evidence of the market’s depth and liquidity: active secondary trading followed the December 2006 issue. Read against Section 15.3.7, it also shows how strongly Sukuk are structured to look like fixed-income bonds.","takeaways":["Global investor base","LIBOR-based pricing","Heavy security package","Fixed-return concern"],"source":{"chapter":15,"section":"15.4","pages":[412,415]},"productIds":["ijarah-sukuk"],"origin":["f1"]},{"id":"c-dm-construction","kind":"textbook","title":"Building a house on the client’s land by DM (Box 12.4)","chapter":12,"topicId":"t12.9.2","modeIds":["diminishing-musharakah"],"scenario":"A client owns a plot worth 1,000,000 dirhams and needs 800,000 to build a house. The bank buys 8 of 10 units of the land (800,000) to create joint ownership and pays the proceeds in four equal instalments. The client builds with the money. When the house is habitable the bank leases its share to the client, who has undertaken to buy the bank’s units on a pre-agreed price schedule.","facts":["Land 1,000,000; bank buys 8 units of 100,000","Disbursement in four instalments of 200,000","Shirkatulmilk with a pre-agreed unit price schedule","Rent on the bank’s share once habitable"],"questions":[{"prompt":"How much is each disbursement instalment?","options":["100,000","200,000","800,000","250,000"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"800,000 ÷ 4 (Box 12.4)."},{"prompt":"When does the bank begin selling its units to the client?","options":["Immediately","One year after the last instalment","When construction starts","After ten years"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"The one-year gap is suggested to avoid Bai‘ al ‘Inah (p. 341)."},{"prompt":"During the first year after the house is ready, what happens to the rent?","options":["It decreases monthly","It stays constant — only rent is paid, since no units are sold yet","It is waived","It doubles"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Box 12.4."},{"prompt":"In a renovation case (client already lives in the house), when does rent start?","options":["After a year","From the first month after the first tranche","Never","At completion only"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Box 12.4."}],"analysis":"The structure is a purchase and lease-back of undivided shares in Shirkatulmilk, so a pre-agreed unit price is allowed. The one-year gap before unit sales keeps the purchase and resale from collapsing into ‘Inah.","takeaways":["Sale and lease-back of land units","Rent from habitation","One-year gap before buy-back","Pre-agreed price allowed in Shirkatulmilk"],"source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.9.2","pages":[340,346]},"productIds":["home-dm","house-construction-dm"],"origin":["f1"]},{"id":"c-wujooh-lc","kind":"textbook","title":"Import finance by Shirkatul Wujooh (Box 12.2)","chapter":12,"topicId":"t12.7.2","modeIds":["musharakah"],"scenario":"A utility awards ABC & Co a contract to supply imported equipment. ABC and an Islamic bank form a partnership in creditworthiness (Shirkatul Wujooh), with no capital from either side. The bank issues a Rs.10 million usance L/C in favour of XYZ Machines, Italy, on 180 days’ credit. The equipment is flown in, cleared in about 30 days and installed in about 50. After the utility’s acceptance test, payment arrives within 150 days of shipment. Profit is shared at the agreed ratio and the bank settles the L/C on the due date.","facts":["Usance L/C Rs.10m, 180 days’ credit","Customs ~30 days; installation ~50 days","Payment within 150 days of shipment","No capital contributed: purchase on credit, sale at spot"],"questions":[{"prompt":"What makes this Shirkatul Wujooh rather than Shirkah al ‘Inan?","options":["The bank lends money","The partners buy on their credit without investing capital, and share the resale profit","It is a Mudarabah","The supplier is foreign"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Section 12.2.2 and Box 12.2."},{"prompt":"Which school holds this form unlawful?","options":["Hanafi","Shafi‘i","Hanbali","All schools"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Hanafis and Hanbalis accept it; Malikis require obligations to be specified first (pp. 310–311)."},{"prompt":"Why is the 180-day credit period important?","options":["It earns interest","Customer payment (within 150 days) arrives before the L/C falls due, so no funding gap arises","It is required by law","It lets the bank charge a time fee"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Box 12.2 timeline."},{"prompt":"If the equipment fails acceptance without ABC’s negligence and the sale yields a loss, who bears it?","options":["ABC only","The partners in proportion to the liability they agreed","The bank only","The supplier"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"In Wujooh, loss is borne as per the liability taken at the beginning (p. 311); security covers only negligence (p. 335)."}],"analysis":"Box 12.2 is the book’s argument that Shirkah can work in trade finance for single transactions. Profit comes from real trade, and the bank shares the risk of the deal.","takeaways":["Partnership in creditworthiness","Credit period matches cash cycle","Loss by agreed liability","Security only against negligence"],"source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.7.2","pages":[332,334]},"productIds":["musharakah-trade"],"origin":["f1"]},{"id":"c-mudarib-capital","kind":"textbook","title":"Bank investing its own funds in a Mudarabah (Section 12.4.1)","chapter":12,"topicId":"t12.4.1","modeIds":["mudarabah"],"scenario":"Depositors provide $2,000 to a Mudarabah and the bank, as Mudarib, adds $1,000 of its own. The agreed Mudarabah ratio is 50:50. The business earns a profit of $300.","facts":["Depositors $2,000; bank $1,000","Mudarabah ratio 50:50","Profit $300"],"questions":[{"prompt":"How much does the bank get as profit on its own capital first?","options":["$150","$100","$200","$300"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Its capital is one-third of the total: 300 × 1/3 = $100 (p. 323)."},{"prompt":"What is the bank’s total profit?","options":["$100","$200","$150","$250"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"$100 on its capital + half of the remaining $200 as Mudarib = $200."},{"prompt":"What do depositors receive?","options":["$200","$100","$150","$300"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Half of $200 = $100."},{"prompt":"If instead there were a loss of $300, who would bear it?","options":["Depositors only","Depositors and bank in proportion to capital — $200 and $100","The bank only, as Mudarib","Nobody"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"A Mudarib who contributes capital bears loss pro rata (p. 326)."}],"analysis":"When the Mudarib invests too, profit is first allocated to capital by proportion, and the rest is shared by the Mudarabah ratio. The bank wears two hats: investor and manager.","takeaways":["Profit on own capital first","Remainder by Mudarabah ratio","Loss by capital"],"source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.4.1","pages":[323,324]},"productIds":[],"origin":["f1"]},{"id":"c-jualah-recovery","kind":"practice","title":"Debt recovery by Ju‘alah (practice — generated for learning)","chapter":13,"topicId":"t13.4.6","modeIds":["juaalah"],"scenario":"An Islamic bank has Rs.20 million of overdue receivables. It signs a Ju‘alah with Recovery Co.: a reward of 4 % of amounts actually collected within 12 months, with Rs.200,000 paid in advance on account. After 5 months the bank cancels the contract. By then Recovery Co. has collected Rs.3 million and done substantial work on the rest.","facts":["Receivables Rs.20m; reward 4 % of collections","Advance Rs.200,000 on account","Cancelled by the bank after 5 months; Rs.3m collected"],"questions":[{"prompt":"Is a reward stated as a percentage of collections valid?","options":["No, it must be a fixed sum","Yes — the reward may be a portion of the realised result","Only if under 1 %","Only for banks"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 352."},{"prompt":"What is Recovery Co. entitled to for the Rs.3m collected?","options":["Nothing","Rs.120,000 (4 % of 3m)","Rs.800,000","Rs.200,000"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"4 % × 3,000,000 (applying p. 352)."},{"prompt":"The bank cancelled after work had begun. What does the book say about the remaining work?","options":["Nothing is payable","The offeror must pay the common market remuneration for work done","Full reward on all Rs.20m","Only the advance"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 353."},{"prompt":"What is the status of the Rs.200,000 advance?","options":["Earned outright","An on-account payment, adjusted against what the worker is entitled to","A penalty","A gift"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 355."}],"analysis":"Ju‘alah fits recovery work because the effort needed is uncertain but the result, cash collected, is measurable. Cancellation after work has begun triggers a market wage; the advance is only on account.","takeaways":["Reward as % of result","Market wage after revocation","Advances on account"],"source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.4.6","pages":[354,355]},"productIds":["jualah-services"],"origin":["f1"]},{"id":"c-tawarruq-check","kind":"practice","title":"Reviewing three Tawarruq proposals (practice — generated for learning)","chapter":13,"topicId":"t13.3","modeIds":["tawarruq"],"scenario":"A bank’s product committee brings three structures to the Shari’ah board. (A) The bank buys copper, sells it to the client on credit, and the client sells it in the market himself. (B) The client is appointed the bank’s agent to buy copper and then sells it to himself on credit. (C) The credit-sale contract states that the bank will act as the client’s agent to sell the copper in the market.","facts":["Three alternative structures","Commodity: copper with an active market","Client needs cash"],"questions":[{"prompt":"What is the ruling on structure A?","options":["Invalid","No Shari’ah problem, if genuine ownership and possession occur","Riba","‘Inah"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 350."},{"prompt":"What is the ruling on structure B?","options":["Valid","Invalid — purchase and sale are interdependent and the bank has not taken possession or risk","Valid but not advisable","Makruh only"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 350."},{"prompt":"What is the ruling on structure C?","options":["Valid","Invalid, because the agency is stipulated in the sale; it would be valid but not advisable if arranged after an unconditional sale","Always recommended","Valid if disclosed"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 350."},{"prompt":"Even for structure A, what policy do the scholars cited recommend?","options":["Unlimited use","Limited use for unavoidable corporate liquidity needs; Kahf would exclude consumers","Use only for credit cards","Replace Musharakah with it"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 350."}],"analysis":"Validity depends on the sequence of ownership, possession and independent contracts. Even a valid Tawarruq raises a policy question: its extensive use could create systemic risk.","takeaways":["Third-party sale only","No self-dealing agency","No stipulated resale agency","Use sparingly"],"source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.3","pages":[349,351]},"productIds":["personal-tawarruq"],"origin":["f1"]},{"id":"c-mudarabah-sukuk-trade","kind":"practice","title":"When may Mudarabah Sukuk trade above par? (practice — generated for learning)","chapter":15,"topicId":"t15.3.5","modeIds":["sukuk"],"scenario":"A Mudarabah Sukuk fund raises Rs.500 million. In month 1 all of it is still cash in the bank. By month 6 it holds Rs.100m cash, Rs.150m receivables and Rs.250m of machinery and inventory. By month 18 it holds Rs.50m cash, Rs.200m receivables and Rs.300m real assets. A holder wants to sell his Sukuk above face value.","facts":["Month 1: 100 % cash","Month 6: 50 % real assets","Month 18: about 55 % real assets"],"questions":[{"prompt":"At month 1, how may the Sukuk be traded?","options":["At any price","Only at face value, under Bai‘ al Sarf rules, as money for money","Not at all","At a discount only"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"OIC Fiqh Council (1988) rules (pp. 398–399)."},{"prompt":"At month 18 the fund holds a mix of cash, receivables and real assets. What pricing rule applies under the OIC resolution?","options":["Face value only","Market price by mutual consent","Debt rules only","Trading banned"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 399."},{"prompt":"Under the more-than-50 % non-liquid test used elsewhere in the book, is month 6 enough?","options":["Yes, 50 % is enough","No — real assets must exceed 50 %; month 18 (about 55 %) passes","Yes, receivables count as real assets","The test does not apply to Sukuk"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Receivables are treated as liquid, like money (pp. 330, 400)."}],"analysis":"The same certificate moves from money, to a mix, to mainly assets, and its permissible pricing changes with it. The book also shows scholars differ on the threshold: above 50 %, some say 33 %, and the Hanafis attach no fixed proportion.","takeaways":["Cash stage: par only","Mixed stage: market price","Receivables count as liquid"],"source":{"chapter":15,"section":"15.3.5","pages":[398,400]},"productIds":["mudarabah-sukuk"],"origin":["f1"]},{"id":"cs-murabaha-1","kind":"scenario","title":"Financing an Imported Steel Order","chapter":9,"topicId":"t9.8","conceptLabel":"Murabaha to Purchase Orderer","modeIds":["murabaha"],"scenario":"A trading company needs 500 tons of steel from a foreign supplier but lacks the funds to pay upfront. It approaches its Islamic bank and promises that, if the bank buys the steel, it will purchase it back from the bank at a markup, payable over six months.","problem":"What financing mode/structure applies here, and what must the bank ensure before it can sell the steel to the client?","prompts":["What must the bank do with the goods before it resells them to the client?","Why can't the bank simply resell steel it has never taken ownership or possession of?"],"answer":"This is Murabaha to Purchase Orderer (MPO): the bank (directly, through a third party, or through the client acting as its agent) purchases the steel and takes ownership and possession of it, and only then executes a separate Murabaha sale to the client at the disclosed cost plus an agreed profit margin, on deferred payment.","explanation":"The book's general conditions of a valid Bai' require the seller to own and possess (actual or constructive) the subject matter before it can be sold (9.2, conditions 4-8), and MPO is described as 'a bunch of contracts' — a promise to purchase, an agency/purchase step, and only then the actual Murabaha sale (9.8, 9.8.1, 9.8.2).","examTakeaway":"In Murabaha the bank must own and bear the risk of the goods before reselling them; executing the sale before that happens is a Bai' al-'Inah problem, not valid Murabaha.","source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.8","pages":[222]},"disclaimer":"Educational scenario for applying Muhammad Ayub's textbook framework — not a Shari'ah ruling or professional financial/legal advice.","productIds":[],"origin":["f2"]},{"id":"cs-murabaha-2","kind":"scenario","title":"Lab Equipment Still Being Configured","chapter":9,"topicId":"t9.2","conceptLabel":"Certainty of Subject Matter in Murabaha","modeIds":["murabaha"],"scenario":"A university asks its Islamic bank to arrange the purchase of specialized lab equipment from an overseas manufacturer and promises to buy it from the bank at a markup once it arrives. However, the exact configuration and final price will only be confirmed after factory testing, weeks after the promise is signed.","problem":"Can the bank execute the actual Murabaha sale to the university now, based only on the promise, or must it wait for something else to happen first?","prompts":["What certainty is required about the subject matter and price before a Bai' can be executed?","What is the legal status of the promise to purchase compared to the actual sale?"],"answer":"The bank cannot execute the Murabaha sale until the equipment exists in its final, specifically identified form, is owned/possessed by the bank, and its price is certain — a promise to purchase is not itself the sale.","explanation":"The general conditions of valid Bai' require the subject matter to exist, be specifically known/identified, and have a certain price stipulated once and for all (9.2, conditions 6, 11, 13); MPO's promise to purchase (9.8.2) is distinct from, and precedes, the actual binding sale.","examTakeaway":"A promise to purchase in MPO creates an obligation to eventually contract, but the Murabaha sale itself can only be executed once the subject matter exists, is owned by the bank, and is fully and specifically defined.","source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.2","pages":[214]},"disclaimer":"Educational scenario for applying Muhammad Ayub's textbook framework — not a Shari'ah ruling or professional financial/legal advice.","productIds":[],"origin":["f2"]},{"id":"cs-salam-1","kind":"scenario","title":"Advance Finance for a Rice Grower","chapter":10,"topicId":"t10.4.2","conceptLabel":"Bai' Salam","modeIds":["salam"],"scenario":"A rice farmer needs cash now to buy seeds and fertilizer, well before his crop is ready for sale. He approaches an Islamic bank offering to deliver a fixed quantity of a specified grade of rice at harvest time in exchange for payment today.","problem":"What financing mode fits this need, and what must be true about the payment for the contract to be valid?","prompts":["When must the bank pay the price, and can any part of it be deferred?","What details about the rice must the contract fix in advance?"],"answer":"This is Bai' Salam: the bank pays the full price in advance, in full, at the time the contract is signed, for a specified quantity, quality and grade of rice to be delivered at an agreed future date and place.","explanation":"Salam exists precisely to meet a producer's need for advance finance (10.2, 10.3); the price (Salam capital) must be paid in full immediately — any deferral turns it into a prohibited sale of debt for debt — and the subject matter must be a clearly specified, fungible commodity (10.4.1, 10.4.2).","examTakeaway":"In Salam, the full price must be paid on the spot; even partial deferral of the price invalidates the contract.","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.4.2","pages":[241,246]},"disclaimer":"Educational scenario for applying Muhammad Ayub's textbook framework — not a Shari'ah ruling or professional financial/legal advice.","productIds":[],"origin":["f2"]},{"id":"cs-salam-2","kind":"scenario","title":"Cotton Delivered Below the Agreed Grade","chapter":10,"topicId":"t10.7","conceptLabel":"Post-Execution Salam: Inferior Goods","modeIds":["salam"],"scenario":"An Islamic bank advanced Salam finance to a cotton grower for a specified quantity and grade of cotton. On the delivery date, the cotton actually supplied is of a noticeably inferior grade to what was contracted.","problem":"What options does the bank have when the seller delivers goods that fall short of the agreed specification?","prompts":["Is the bank obliged to accept goods that don't meet the agreed grade?","What alternatives does the bank have besides outright rejection?"],"answer":"The bank is not obliged to accept inferior goods; it may refuse and insist on the agreed goods (or recover its price), it may accept the inferior goods at a discounted price, or — if the seller genuinely cannot supply the agreed item and it is unavailable in the market — request a substitute or a partial-supply adjustment.","explanation":"This is exactly the scenario the book addresses under 'Supply of Inferior Goods' (10.7.3), which lists refusal, discounted acceptance, substitution, and partial-supply adjustment as the bank's available remedies.","examTakeaway":"A Salam buyer is never forced to accept goods below the contracted specification — the book gives the bank a structured menu of remedies rather than leaving it to simply absorb the loss.","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.7.3","pages":[253]},"disclaimer":"Educational scenario for applying Muhammad Ayub's textbook framework — not a Shari'ah ruling or professional financial/legal advice.","productIds":[],"origin":["f2"]},{"id":"cs-istisnaa-1","kind":"scenario","title":"Financing a Custom Overpass Construction","chapter":10,"topicId":"t10.11.3","conceptLabel":"Istisna'a Payment Flexibility","modeIds":["istisnaa"],"scenario":"A construction firm has won a contract to build a custom overpass for a city government but has no upfront capital for materials and labor. It needs financing that can be released progressively as construction advances, rather than all at once.","problem":"Which forward-sale mode fits a build-to-order project like this, and why is it better suited than an alternative like Salam?","prompts":["Why would Salam's payment rule be unworkable for a custom construction project?","What payment flexibility does the correct mode offer instead?"],"answer":"This calls for Istisna'a: the subject matter (the overpass) needs to be manufactured/constructed to order, and the price can be paid immediately, deferred, or in instalments tied to progress — unlike Salam, which requires full advance payment.","explanation":"Istisna'a's subject matter is, by definition, always something that needs manufacturing (10.11.1), and its price does not need to be paid in full in advance, giving the flexibility a progressive-payment construction project needs (10.11.3).","examTakeaway":"Istisna'a's flexible, progress-linked payment schedule — unlike Salam's mandatory full advance payment — is what makes it the natural fit for manufacturing and construction financing.","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.11.3","pages":[263,265]},"disclaimer":"Educational scenario for applying Muhammad Ayub's textbook framework — not a Shari'ah ruling or professional financial/legal advice.","productIds":[],"origin":["f2"]},{"id":"cs-istisnaa-2","kind":"scenario","title":"Backing Out of a Parallel Istisna'a Shipbuilding Deal","chapter":10,"topicId":"t10.11.5","conceptLabel":"Binding Nature of Istisna'a","modeIds":["istisnaa"],"scenario":"An Islamic bank signs an Istisna'a contract with a shipbuilder to construct a vessel to a shipping company's specifications, intending to sell the finished ship to that shipping company under a Parallel Istisna'a. Before the shipbuilder starts any work, the shipping company wants to withdraw from the deal.","problem":"Is the shipping company free to cancel at this stage, and would the answer change once construction has begun?","prompts":["What determines whether an Istisna'a contract is binding or not?","Why must the two Istisna'a contracts in a Parallel Istisna'a structure stay independent of each other?"],"answer":"Istisna'a is nonbinding as long as the manufacturer has not started work — either party may cancel by giving notice. Once the manufacturer begins work, the contract becomes binding and cannot be cancelled unilaterally by the buyer; each leg of a Parallel Istisna'a must also remain a separate, independently enforceable contract.","explanation":"This is the explicit rule under 'The Binding Nature of an Istisna'a Contract' (10.11.5) and 'Parallel Contract — Subcontracting' (10.11.7), where ownership-related risks in the two legs are kept separate and neither contract's rights/obligations may be made dependent on the other.","examTakeaway":"Istisna'a's bindingness turns on a precise timing rule: whether the manufacturer has actually started work — a distinction commonly tested against Salam's immediate bindingness.","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.11.5","pages":[266,267]},"disclaimer":"Educational scenario for applying Muhammad Ayub's textbook framework — not a Shari'ah ruling or professional financial/legal advice.","productIds":[],"origin":["f2"]},{"id":"cs-ijarah-1","kind":"scenario","title":"Leasing Delivery Trucks Toward Eventual Ownership","chapter":11,"topicId":"t11.4.5","conceptLabel":"Ijarah Muntahia-bi-Tamleek","modeIds":["ijarah-muntahia-bi-tamleek","ijarah"],"scenario":"A logistics company wants to acquire a fleet of delivery trucks through an Islamic bank's leasing facility and, after several years of rental payments, wants to end up owning the trucks outright rather than returning them.","problem":"How can the lease agreement be structured so that ownership eventually transfers, without violating the Shari'ah rule against combining two contracts in one bargain?","prompts":["Why can't the lease agreement simply state upfront that ownership transfers automatically at the end?","What kind of promise can the bank make instead, and what makes it different from a binding sale clause?"],"answer":"The bank structures this as Ijarah Muntahia-bi-Tamleek: a standard Ijarah lease that runs alongside a separate, independent, unilateral promise by the bank (as lessor) to sell or gift the trucks to the company at the end of the lease term.","explanation":"The book explains that the lease and the ownership-transfer promise must be kept as two separate, independently enforceable contracts, with the promise binding only the promisor (the bank), to avoid the prohibited combination of 'two contracts in one bargain' (11.4.5).","examTakeaway":"A Shari'ah-compliant lease-to-own arrangement keeps the lease contract and the unilateral promise to transfer ownership legally separate — never a single, mutually binding combined contract.","source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.4.5","pages":[289,290]},"disclaimer":"Educational scenario for applying Muhammad Ayub's textbook framework — not a Shari'ah ruling or professional financial/legal advice.","productIds":[],"origin":["f2"]},{"id":"cs-ijarah-2","kind":"scenario","title":"Leased Machinery Breaks Down From a Manufacturing Defect","chapter":11,"topicId":"t11.3.2","conceptLabel":"Ownership Risk and Rent Entitlement in Ijarah","modeIds":["ijarah"],"scenario":"A factory leases specialized machinery from an Islamic bank under an Ijarah contract. Midway through the lease, the machinery stops working because of a manufacturing defect, through no fault of the factory (lessee).","problem":"Who bears the repair cost, and is the factory still obligated to pay rent while the machinery cannot be used?","prompts":["Who is responsible for defects that prevent the lessee from using the asset?","What happens to the rent obligation if the usufruct is lost without any misuse by the lessee?"],"answer":"The bank, as lessor, must bear the cost of repairing the defect, and it cannot claim rent for the period the machinery is unusable, since the loss of usufruct occurred without any misuse or negligence by the factory.","explanation":"The book states that expenses to rectify defects preventing use are the lessor's responsibility while the lessee handles only day-to-day running costs, and explicitly notes that if the leased asset loses its usufruct without any misuse or negligence by the lessee, the lessor cannot claim the rent and must bear the loss.","examTakeaway":"The defining test of a genuine Ijarah (versus disguised interest-based lease financing) is whether the lessor truly bears the asset's ownership risk — including forfeiting rent when usufruct is lost through no fault of the lessee.","source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.3.2","pages":[283,284]},"disclaimer":"Educational scenario for applying Muhammad Ayub's textbook framework — not a Shari'ah ruling or professional financial/legal advice.","productIds":[],"origin":["f2"]},{"id":"cs-musharakah-1","kind":"scenario","title":"Two Partners, Unequal Roles, in an Import-Export Venture","chapter":12,"topicId":"t12.3.4","conceptLabel":"Musharakah Profit/Loss Ratios","modeIds":["murabaha","musharakah"],"scenario":"Two entrepreneurs start an import-export trading business together, contributing capital in a 60:40 ratio. One partner will be actively running day-to-day operations while the other remains largely a sleeping partner.","problem":"Can the partners agree to split profits differently from their capital ratio to compensate the active partner, and how must any loss be shared?","prompts":["What flexibility exists in agreeing a profit-sharing ratio compared to the capital ratio?","Does the same flexibility apply to how a loss is shared?"],"answer":"The partners may freely agree a profit-sharing ratio that differs from their capital ratio (e.g. rewarding the active partner with a larger share), but any loss must always be shared strictly in proportion to each partner's capital contribution.","explanation":"The book states the general ruling (from Imam Abu Hanifa and Imam Ahmad) that the profit ratio may differ from the investment ratio based on the work a partner contributes, while there is complete jurist consensus that loss must always be shared exactly according to the ratio of capital invested (12.3.4).","examTakeaway":"In Musharakah, 'profit is based on agreement of the parties, but loss is always subject to the ratio of investment' — a maxim worth memorizing verbatim for exams.","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.3.4","pages":[316,317]},"disclaimer":"Educational scenario for applying Muhammad Ayub's textbook framework — not a Shari'ah ruling or professional financial/legal advice.","productIds":[],"origin":["f2"]},{"id":"cs-musharakah-2","kind":"scenario","title":"A Bank Wants to Raise Its Profit Share Mid-Project","chapter":12,"topicId":"t12.3.4","conceptLabel":"Amending a Musharakah Agreement","modeIds":["musharakah"],"scenario":"An Islamic bank enters a Musharakah with a property developer for a real-estate project. Midway through construction, citing rising costs, the bank unilaterally tells the developer it will now take a larger share of the eventual profit than originally agreed.","problem":"Can the bank make this change on its own, or is something else required?","prompts":["When must the profit-sharing ratio be fixed, and can it later be amended?","What is required for any amendment to be valid?"],"answer":"No — the bank cannot unilaterally alter the agreed profit ratio; the ratio must be agreed without ambiguity at the time of contract execution, and any later amendment requires the mutual consent of all partners, with loss-sharing still tracking the capital ratio.","explanation":"The book requires the profit ratio to be settled at execution (otherwise the contract is invalid) and allows amendment of partnership terms only by mutual agreement, while loss must continue to follow the capital ratio (12.3.4).","examTakeaway":"A Musharakah partner cannot impose a change to the profit-sharing ratio unilaterally — any amendment needs the consent of all partners.","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.3.4","pages":[316,317]},"disclaimer":"Educational scenario for applying Muhammad Ayub's textbook framework — not a Shari'ah ruling or professional financial/legal advice.","productIds":[],"origin":["f2"]},{"id":"cs-mudarabah-1","kind":"scenario","title":"A Trading Venture Ends the Year at a Loss","chapter":12,"topicId":"t12.4","conceptLabel":"Loss-Bearing in Mudarabah","modeIds":["mudarabah"],"scenario":"An investor (Rabbul-mal) provides capital to a trader (Mudarib) to run a Halal trading venture on a 70:30 profit-sharing agreement. Due to a normal market downturn — with no negligence or misconduct by the trader — the venture ends the year at a loss.","problem":"Who bears this financial loss, and does the trader owe the investor anything for his own time and effort?","prompts":["What is the general rule on who bears a Mudarabah's financial loss?","When, if ever, does the Mudarib become liable for a loss?"],"answer":"The financial loss (shortfall in capital) is borne exclusively by the Rabbul-mal; the Mudarib's only 'loss' is his uncompensated time and effort. The Mudarib becomes liable only if the loss resulted from his negligence, dishonesty, or breach of the contract's conditions.","explanation":"The book states that in Mudarabah, profit is shared per the pre-agreed proportion while loss is borne exclusively by the investor; the Mudarib is not liable for loss in the normal course of business, only for loss caused by his own negligence or misconduct (12.4).","examTakeaway":"In Mudarabah, ordinary business-risk losses fall entirely on the capital provider — never on the working partner, absent proven negligence or misconduct.","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.4","pages":[320,321]},"disclaimer":"Educational scenario for applying Muhammad Ayub's textbook framework — not a Shari'ah ruling or professional financial/legal advice.","productIds":[],"origin":["f2"]},{"id":"cs-mudarabah-2","kind":"scenario","title":"A Mudarib Wants to Delegate to a Sub-Manager","chapter":12,"topicId":"t12.4","conceptLabel":"Sub-Mudarabah","modeIds":["mudarabah"],"scenario":"A bank pools depositors' funds under a Mudarabah arrangement and appoints a Mudarib to manage an investment business with the funds. Partway through, the Mudarib wants to hand part of the business over to a sub-manager, without telling the bank.","problem":"Is the Mudarib allowed to do this on his own initiative?","prompts":["Under what condition can a Mudarib pass capital on to a sub-Mudarib?","What is the status of the arrangement if that condition is not met?"],"answer":"No — a Mudarib may only place the Mudarabah capital with a third party on Mudarabah terms if the original financier (Rabbul-mal) has permitted it, either explicitly or by leaving the conduct of the business to the Mudarib's discretion; without that permission, the sub-Mudarabah is voidable.","explanation":"The book notes jurists' unanimity that a sub-Mudarabah requires the financier's permission, in clear terms or by leaving discretion to the Mudarib, and that its absence makes the arrangement voidable (12.4).","examTakeaway":"A Mudarib cannot unilaterally sub-delegate the managed capital — authorization from the Rabbul-mal, explicit or by delegated discretion, is a precondition for a valid sub-Mudarabah.","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.4","pages":[321,322]},"disclaimer":"Educational scenario for applying Muhammad Ayub's textbook framework — not a Shari'ah ruling or professional financial/legal advice.","productIds":[],"origin":["f2"]},{"id":"cs-diminishing-musharakah-1","kind":"scenario","title":"Buying a Family Home Without a Conventional Mortgage","chapter":12,"topicId":"t12.9.2","conceptLabel":"Diminishing Musharakah for Home Financing","modeIds":["diminishing-musharakah","musharakah"],"scenario":"A family wants to buy a house through their Islamic bank without taking on an interest-based mortgage. They will live in the house immediately and make monthly payments to the bank until they own it outright.","problem":"What structure lets the bank finance the purchase while the family gradually becomes the sole owner?","prompts":["What separate contracts need to be signed, and in what order?","What happens to the monthly payment as the family buys more of the bank's share?"],"answer":"This is Diminishing Musharakah through partnership by ownership (Shirkatulmilk): the bank and the family become co-owners via a Musharakah agreement, the bank leases its undivided share to the family for rent under a separate Ijarah agreement, and the family periodically buys units of the bank's share per a pre-agreed price schedule — with the rent decreasing as the family's ownership share grows.","explanation":"The book sets out this exact sequence — joint ownership, then a rent agreement, then a unilateral undertaking to purchase units on a pre-agreed schedule — insisting the three contracts remain separate and independently enforceable (12.9, 12.9.2).","examTakeaway":"In DM home financing, the Musharakah (joint ownership), Ijarah (lease), and unit-purchase undertaking must be kept as three separate, independently enforceable contracts to remain Shari'ah-compliant.","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.9.2","pages":[339,340]},"disclaimer":"Educational scenario for applying Muhammad Ayub's textbook framework — not a Shari'ah ruling or professional financial/legal advice.","productIds":[],"origin":["f2"]},{"id":"cs-diminishing-musharakah-2","kind":"scenario","title":"Pre-Fixing a Buyout Price in a Trading Partnership","chapter":12,"topicId":"t12.9.1","conceptLabel":"Diminishing Musharakah in Trade","modeIds":["diminishing-musharakah","musharakah"],"scenario":"Two partners start a retail trading business through Diminishing Musharakah on a 40:60 basis, with the smaller partner intending to gradually buy out the larger (financier) partner's share. They want to fix today the exact price per ownership unit that will apply to every future purchase.","problem":"Can the partners validly pre-fix the buyout price for a trading-business Diminishing Musharakah the way they could for a house?","prompts":["Why might pre-fixing the price of ownership units amount to a prohibited capital guarantee?","How should the units instead be priced at each purchase?"],"answer":"No — because this is Diminishing Musharakah in trade (entered into for profit-earning, with no leasing involved), the price of ownership units cannot be fixed in advance; each unit must instead be priced by valuation (market value or an agreed expert valuation) at the time of that particular purchase.","explanation":"The book explains that pre-fixing the unit price in a trade-based DM would effectively mean the buying partner has guaranteed the financier partner's invested capital, which is prohibited; pre-agreed pricing is permitted only in DM through partnership by ownership (e.g. housing), not in DM for trade (12.8, 12.9.1).","examTakeaway":"Unlike DM for housing/fixed-asset financing, where a pre-agreed purchase-price schedule is permitted, DM in a trading partnership must price each unit sale by current valuation — pre-fixing it would be an impermissible capital guarantee.","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.9.1","pages":[337,339]},"disclaimer":"Educational scenario for applying Muhammad Ayub's textbook framework — not a Shari'ah ruling or professional financial/legal advice.","productIds":[],"origin":["f2"]},{"id":"cs-sukuk-1","kind":"scenario","title":"Financing a Toll Road Without Interest-Bearing Bonds","chapter":15,"topicId":"t15.3.1","conceptLabel":"Asset-Backed Sukuk via an SPV","modeIds":["sukuk"],"scenario":"A government wants to raise long-term funds for a new toll-road project but wishes to avoid issuing conventional interest-bearing bonds.","problem":"How can the government structure a Shari'ah-compliant issue that still lets many investors participate, and what must genuinely happen to the underlying asset for it to be valid?","prompts":["What entity typically issues the certificates, and what does it hold?","What condition must be satisfied for a toll-road (future-flow) securitization to be Shari'ah-compliant?"],"answer":"The government can securitize the toll road through a Special Purpose Vehicle (SPV), which issues Sukuk representing investors' pro-rata undivided ownership in the road; investors earn returns from the toll revenue/cash flow the asset generates, not a fixed interest coupon — and, for Shari'ah compliance, actual pro-rata ownership of the asset must genuinely transfer to the Sukuk holders.","explanation":"The book lists road-toll securitization under 'future flow securitization' and specifically notes that transfer of pro rata ownership to Sukuk holders is necessary for its Shari'ah compliance, with the SPV set up to manage assets and issue certificates on the holders' behalf (15.3.1).","examTakeaway":"For toll/road or lease-rental securitization to be Shari'ah-compliant, genuine pro-rata ownership of the underlying asset — not merely a claim on its cash flow — must pass to the Sukuk holders.","source":{"chapter":15,"section":"15.3.1","pages":[393,394]},"disclaimer":"Educational scenario for applying Muhammad Ayub's textbook framework — not a Shari'ah ruling or professional financial/legal advice.","productIds":[],"origin":["f2"]},{"id":"cs-sukuk-2","kind":"scenario","title":"An Issuer Wants to Guarantee a Fixed Sukuk Return","chapter":15,"topicId":"t15.3.5","conceptLabel":"No Capital or Profit Guarantee in Mudarabah/Musharakah Sukuk","modeIds":["musharakah","mudarabah","sukuk"],"scenario":"An investment bank plans to issue Mudarabah Sukuk to fund a diversified business venture. To make the issue more attractive, it wants to guarantee investors a fixed percentage return on their capital regardless of how the venture actually performs.","problem":"Is this guarantee permissible for a Mudarabah-based Sukuk issue?","prompts":["What does the Fiqh Council resolution the book cites say about guaranteeing capital or a fixed profit in Mudarabah Sukuk?","How should the profit actually be distributed instead?"],"answer":"No — neither the prospectus nor the Sukuk may contain a guarantee, from the issuer or fund manager, of the capital or of a fixed profit or a profit based on any percentage of the capital; profit must instead be divided as an amount in excess of capital, determined by Shari'ah rules, once the Profit and Loss Account is published to holders.","explanation":"The book lists this as one of the salient features of Mudarabah Sukuk from the 1988 OIC Fiqh Council resolution: no guarantee of capital or fixed/percentage profit by the issuer or manager is permitted (15.3.5).","examTakeaway":"A Mudarabah/Musharakah-based Sukuk cannot legitimately guarantee its holders a fixed return or capital protection from the issuer — doing so would recreate Riba in securitized form.","source":{"chapter":15,"section":"15.3.5","pages":[398,399]},"disclaimer":"Educational scenario for applying Muhammad Ayub's textbook framework — not a Shari'ah ruling or professional financial/legal advice.","productIds":[],"origin":["f2"]},{"id":"cs-takaful-1","kind":"scenario","title":"Insuring a Warehouse Without Conventional Insurance","chapter":16,"topicId":"t16.2.1","conceptLabel":"Why Takaful Avoids Riba, Gharar and Maisir","modeIds":["takaful"],"scenario":"A small business owner wants cover against fire damage to his warehouse but is reluctant to use a conventional insurer because of concerns about Riba, Gharar and Maisir in how premiums and payouts work.","problem":"What alternative structure addresses these specific concerns, and how does it avoid them?","prompts":["What happens to a participant's contribution in Takaful, structurally, that differs from a conventional premium?","Why does that structural difference avoid Riba, Gharar and Maisir?"],"answer":"Takaful is the alternative: the business owner's contribution is treated as a donation (Tabarru') into a common fund (often structured as a Waqf), from which losses to any member of the group are paid, with members mutually sharing any underwriting surplus or loss — rather than transferring risk to a company for a price.","explanation":"The book explains that conventional insurance is questioned mainly because of the Riba, Gharar and gambling elements in the premium-for-uncertain-benefit exchange (16.2.1), whereas Takaful reframes the same need for risk cover as a mutual donation and risk-sharing arrangement among participants (16.5).","examTakeaway":"What makes Takaful Shari'ah-compliant is not the absence of risk-pooling — it is that participants donate (Tabarru') into a shared fund and mutually indemnify one another, rather than transferring risk to an insurer for a price.","source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.2.1","pages":[418]},"disclaimer":"Educational scenario for applying Muhammad Ayub's textbook framework — not a Shari'ah ruling or professional financial/legal advice.","productIds":[],"origin":["f2"]},{"id":"cs-takaful-2","kind":"scenario","title":"What Happens to a Year's Underwriting Surplus","chapter":16,"topicId":"t16.5","conceptLabel":"Underwriting Surplus in Takaful","modeIds":["takaful"],"scenario":"A Takaful operator manages a family Takaful fund on behalf of a group of participants. At the end of the year, the risk fund shows an underwriting surplus after all claims have been paid.","problem":"Who is entitled to that surplus — the operator/company, or the participants?","prompts":["How does the book's preferred Takaful model treat underwriting surplus, and how is it distributed?","How does this differ from what happens to underwriting profit at a conventional insurer?"],"answer":"In the model the book considers best, the underwriting surplus (and underwriting loss, UWS/UWL) fully belongs to the participants, and is distributed among them pro rata to their contributions — the Takaful company only shares in any investment profit, managed separately.","explanation":"The book states that the Takaful model in which UWS/UWL fully belongs to the participants is considered the best model per the latest research, with surplus going back to participants in proportion to their contributions (16.5).","examTakeaway":"Unlike a conventional insurer, which keeps underwriting profit as shareholder profit, best-practice Takaful returns any underwriting surplus to participants in proportion to their contributions.","source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.5","pages":[427]},"disclaimer":"Educational scenario for applying Muhammad Ayub's textbook framework — not a Shari'ah ruling or professional financial/legal advice.","productIds":[],"origin":["f2"]},{"id":"cs-wakalah-1","kind":"scenario","title":"A Fund Manager Wants a Bigger Cut Mid-Year","chapter":13,"topicId":"t13.2.2","conceptLabel":"Wakalatul Istismar (Investment Agency) Fee Structure","modeIds":["wakalah"],"scenario":"An Islamic bank manages a pool of investors' funds under a Wakalatul Istismar arrangement, earning a pre-agreed 2% annual fee based on the fund's net asset value. Midway through the year, citing a strong quarter, the bank wants to additionally take a percentage of the profit earned, on top of its disclosed fee, without amending the prospectus or informing investors in advance.","problem":"Can the bank add this extra profit-based charge on its own initiative, and how should a Wakalah-based fund manager's compensation actually be structured and disclosed?","prompts":["What must be determined about the manager's fee before the fund is even launched?","Does a pure agency (Wakalah) fee depend on whether the fund makes a profit or a loss?"],"answer":"No — under Wakalatul Istismar the bank's compensation must be a pre-agreed fee (a lump sum, or a percentage of the investment amount/net asset value) that is fixed and disclosed in the fund's prospectus before its launch, and it is earned irrespective of whether the portfolio makes a profit or a loss. The bank cannot unilaterally add an undisclosed, profit-linked charge partway through the year; all profit or loss on the pooled funds otherwise passes through to the investors.","explanation":"The book states that under Wakalatul Istismar, banks get a pre-agreed fee irrespective of the profit or loss on the relevant portfolio, fixed as a lump sum or as a percentage of the investment amount/net asset value, and that it is necessary to determine this method before the fund's launch and disclose it in the prospectus — subscribers are presumed to agree to whatever terms the prospectus discloses (13.2.2).","examTakeaway":"A Wakalah-based fund manager's compensation is a fixed, pre-disclosed fee unrelated to profit or loss — introducing a later, undisclosed profit-based charge breaks the Wakalah structure's basic requirement that the agency fee be settled and disclosed before the fund launches.","source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.2.2","pages":[349]},"disclaimer":"Educational scenario for applying Muhammad Ayub's textbook framework — not a Shari'ah ruling or professional financial/legal advice.","productIds":[],"origin":["f2"]},{"id":"cs-jualah-1","kind":"scenario","title":"Recovering a Portfolio of Defaulted Business Loans","chapter":13,"topicId":"t13.4.6","conceptLabel":"Ju'alah for Debt Recovery","modeIds":["juaalah"],"scenario":"An Islamic bank holds a portfolio of overdue, hard-to-collect debts from small business clients and wants to engage a specialist recovery agent. Neither the bank nor the agent can specify in advance exactly how much work recovery will take, or whether any given debt will be recovered at all.","problem":"Why is an ordinary Ijarah (hiring) contract unworkable here, and what mode should the bank use instead to engage the recovery agent?","prompts":["Why does a valid Ijarah contract require the work itself to be specified in advance?","How is the recovery agent's entitlement to a reward determined under the correct mode, and when is it earned?"],"answer":"The bank should use Ju'alah rather than Ijarah: it offers the agent (the 'worker') a specified reward — for example, a percentage of the amount actually recovered — contingent on realizing the result (debt collected), not on hours worked or effort expended. Ju'alah is explicitly suited to activities like this that Ijarah cannot handle, because Ijarah requires the work itself to be minutely specified in advance.","explanation":"The book explains that Ju'alah is relevant precisely for events that cannot be accomplished through Ijarah because the required work cannot be specified in advance, and gives 'recovery of overdue debts' as a named example, with the reward payable as a percentage of the amount collected and the worker not entitled to any reward for effort alone if the required result is not realized (13.4, 13.4.6).","examTakeaway":"Ju'alah's defining feature — a reward contingent on realizing an uncertain result rather than on performing specified work — is exactly why it, not Ijarah, is the correct mode for open-ended tasks like debt recovery.","source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.4.6","pages":[354,355]},"disclaimer":"Educational scenario for applying Muhammad Ayub's textbook framework — not a Shari'ah ruling or professional financial/legal advice.","productIds":[],"origin":["f2"]},{"id":"cs-riba-1","kind":"scenario","title":"A 'Free' Loan With a Favor Attached","chapter":3,"topicId":"t3.2.1","conceptLabel":"Riba: Any Stipulated Benefit on a Loan","modeIds":[],"scenario":"A wholesaler agrees to lend a struggling retailer Rs. 500,000 with no interest charged. As a condition of the loan, however, the retailer must let the wholesaler store his own excess inventory in the retailer's warehouse, rent-free, for as long as the loan is outstanding — something the two had never done before this loan.","problem":"Is this loan free of Riba simply because no interest rate is charged, or is something else going on that makes it problematic?","prompts":["Does Riba require an explicit interest rate, or can it take the form of any stipulated benefit to the lender?","Would the answer differ if the two parties had always swapped this kind of favor with each other, loan or no loan?"],"answer":"This loan is not free of Riba. The free warehouse space is a benefit flowing to the lender (wholesaler) as a precondition of the loan, over and above the principal — that is Riba, regardless of the fact that no interest rate was named.","explanation":"The book reports the Prophet's (pbuh) instruction that a lender should not even accept a gift or a ride offered by the borrower unless such an exchange of favors was already customary between them before the loan (Hadith from Anas ibn Malik, 3.2.1), and gives the broader rule that Riba means any increase over and above the principal of a loan or debt, in quantity or in kind, not corresponding to a genuine trade, labour, risk or expertise on the lender's part.","examTakeaway":"Riba is not limited to a named interest rate — any benefit a lender stipulates for himself as a condition of the loan, however small or non-monetary, is Riba unless that exchange of favors already existed independently of the loan.","source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","pages":[46,48]},"disclaimer":"Educational scenario for applying Muhammad Ayub's textbook framework — not a Shari'ah ruling or professional financial/legal advice.","productIds":[],"origin":["f2"]},{"id":"cs-maisir-1","kind":"scenario","title":"A Savings Scheme With a Monthly Prize Draw","chapter":3,"topicId":"t3.2.3","conceptLabel":"Maisir/Qimar in Prize-Linked Savings Schemes","modeIds":[],"scenario":"A savings institution advertises a scheme in which depositors' principal is fully safe and guaranteed. Each month, the institution takes the interest income earned on the pooled deposits and uses it to fund a lottery-style drawing: a handful of randomly selected depositors win large cash prizes, while everyone else gets nothing beyond their untouched principal.","problem":"Does this scheme raise a Shari'ah concern, and if so, under which prohibition, given that no depositor's principal is ever at risk?","prompts":["Where does the prize money in this scheme actually come from?","What distinguishes a prohibited prize-draw scheme from a permissible sales promotion where a known, fixed incentive is given to every purchaser?"],"answer":"This scheme is repugnant to the Shari'ah on account of Maisir/Qimar (and, in the underlying interest income, Riba): even though the depositors' capital is intact, the prizes are distributed by chance from interest generated on the pooled capital, meaning some depositors gain at the expense of others through a game of chance rather than any real return on their own funds.","explanation":"The book specifically addresses this pattern: a scheme wherein the investors' money is safe and intact, but the prizes given are related to interest generated from the capital accumulated through it, is repugnant to the tenets of the Shari'ah due to the involvement of Qimar — distinguishing it from a valid promotional incentive, where the price and the extra item given to every purchaser are both known in advance with no element of chance (3.2.3).","examTakeaway":"A prize scheme is not saved from Maisir just because depositors' principal is guaranteed — if the prize pool itself is distributed by chance rather than known to and shared by all participants, it is still gambling, and worse here because the pool is funded by Riba.","source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.3","pages":[62,63]},"disclaimer":"Educational scenario for applying Muhammad Ayub's textbook framework — not a Shari'ah ruling or professional financial/legal advice.","productIds":[],"origin":["f2"]},{"id":"cs-bai-inah-1","kind":"scenario","title":"Quick Cash Through a Sell-and-Buy-Back Deal","chapter":6,"topicId":"t6.11","conceptLabel":"Bai' al-'Inah (Sale and Buy-Back)","modeIds":[],"scenario":"A trading company needs cash urgently. A financier proposes: it will buy the company's machine today for $95,000 cash, and the company will simultaneously agree to buy the same machine back from the financier in six months for $100,000, payable then. No one else is involved, and the machine never actually changes hands or location.","problem":"Does this arrangement amount to legitimate Islamic trade financing, or does it fall foul of a specific prohibited structure?","prompts":["What is the real economic substance of buying an asset for less cash today and selling it back for more, later, to the same party?","Why does it matter that the asset never actually moves or changes possession?"],"answer":"This is Bai' al-'Inah, a prohibited double sale involving buy-back, in which the same commodity is sold and resold between the same two parties — once for cash at a lower price, once on credit at a higher price — with the net economic effect of a cash loan repaid with an increase, i.e. Riba in the form of trade.","explanation":"The book gives essentially this exact structure as unlawful: a Hilah in which a commodity is sold for $1000 payable after a year and then bought back for $950 cash was declared unlawful due to the involvement of Riba, and describes Bai' al-'Inah as a double sale by which the borrower and lender sell and resell a commodity between them, once for cash and once for a higher credit price, with the net result of a loan carrying interest — a structure the majority of jurists (and Shari'ah boards such as Al Baraka's) have prohibited (6.11).","examTakeaway":"The test for Bai' al-'Inah is substance over form: if a 'sale' and its 'buy-back' between the same two parties simply recreate a cash loan repaid with an increase, it is Riba dressed as trade, regardless of the sale-contract paperwork used.","source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.11","pages":[148,149]},"disclaimer":"Educational scenario for applying Muhammad Ayub's textbook framework — not a Shari'ah ruling or professional financial/legal advice.","productIds":[],"origin":["f2"]},{"id":"cs-khiyar-1","kind":"scenario","title":"Defective Printing Equipment Delivered Under MPO","chapter":6,"topicId":"t6.12","conceptLabel":"Khiyar al-'Aib (Option of Defect) in Murabaha to Purchase Orderer","modeIds":["murabaha"],"scenario":"A publishing company ordered specialized printing equipment through its Islamic bank's Murabaha to Purchase Orderer facility, promising to buy it from the bank once delivered. When the equipment arrives and the Murabaha sale to the client is executed, the client discovers a material defect that was not disclosed and does not match the specifications originally agreed.","problem":"Is the client bound to complete the purchase and simply live with the defect, or does the client have any recourse under the sale contract?","prompts":["What general option does a buyer have when goods are found to be defective or not as specified, even without a special clause in the contract?","Does the client's earlier promise to purchase remove this option once the actual Murabaha sale has been executed?"],"answer":"The client is not bound to accept defective or non-conforming equipment. In an MPO transaction the client retains the options of defect and specification/quality: if the goods are not per the stipulated specification or have a material defect, the client may refuse to purchase them under the promise, and if the Murabaha sale has already been executed, the client has the right to rescind it — unless the bank obtained a certificate of fitness at the time of sale after giving the client a real opportunity to examine the asset.","explanation":"The book states this general rule as Khiyar al-'Aib: goods can be returned if found defective, and this option is available even without an explicit contractual stipulation as long as the defect was not disclosed to the buyer and caused a visible decrease in value; it then applies this directly to MPO, giving the client options of defect and specification/quality over the delivered asset (6.12).","examTakeaway":"Khiyar al-'Aib is not something a buyer must remember to write into the contract — it exists by default whenever an undisclosed defect materially reduces the value of what was sold, including in a Murabaha to Purchase Orderer sale.","source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.12","pages":[150,152]},"disclaimer":"Educational scenario for applying Muhammad Ayub's textbook framework — not a Shari'ah ruling or professional financial/legal advice.","productIds":[],"origin":["f2"]},{"id":"cs-hawalah-1","kind":"scenario","title":"Raising Cash by Offloading a Receivable","chapter":7,"topicId":"t7.14","conceptLabel":"Hawalah vs the Prohibited Sale of Debt","modeIds":["hawalah"],"scenario":"A construction company is owed $100,000 by a government agency, due in 90 days. Needing cash now, it wants to transfer this receivable to an investor for $90,000 upfront, with the investor bearing the full loss if the government agency ultimately fails to pay — the company would have no further obligation either way.","problem":"Can the company structure this transfer in a Shari'ah-compliant way, and if so, what would have to change?","prompts":["What is the key difference between an outright 'sale of debt' and a valid Hawalah (assignment of debt)?","At what value can a debt validly be assigned under Hawalah, and what happens if the new debtor fails to pay?"],"answer":"Selling the receivable at a discount with no recourse to the company is a prohibited sale of debt (it involves Gharar and Riba). A Shari'ah-compliant alternative is Hawalah: the company can assign the debt to the investor, but only at its full nominal (face) value, not at a discount, and the assignor (the company) remains liable if the assignee/new debtor ultimately fails to collect from the government agency.","explanation":"The book draws exactly this distinction: the difference between a prohibited 'sale of debt' and a permissible 'assignment of debt' (Hawalah) is that in Hawalah there is recourse to the assignor/original debtor if the assignee does not pay, whereas in a sale of debt the purchaser has no such recourse; consequently, Hawalah is allowed only at the nominal value of the debt, with recourse to the assignor if the assignee defaults (7.14).","examTakeaway":"A discounted, no-recourse transfer of a receivable is a prohibited sale of debt; a Shari'ah-compliant Hawalah instead requires transfer at full face value with recourse back to the original creditor's debtor if the new party doesn't pay.","source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.14","pages":[167,168]},"disclaimer":"Educational scenario for applying Muhammad Ayub's textbook framework — not a Shari'ah ruling or professional financial/legal advice.","productIds":[],"origin":["f2"]},{"id":"cs-default-penalty-1","kind":"scenario","title":"Can the Bank Keep the Late-Payment Fine?","chapter":7,"topicId":"t7.13","conceptLabel":"Penalty on Wilful Default — Charity, Not Bank Income","modeIds":[],"scenario":"A Murabaha client who is clearly able to pay keeps deliberately delaying his instalments. The Islamic bank wants to impose a late-payment penalty clause, and its finance department proposes booking the collected penalty as extra income to help offset the bank's own losses from such defaults.","problem":"Can the bank charge this penalty, and can it keep the proceeds as its own income?","prompts":["Under what conditions have Shari'ah scholars allowed a penalty clause for default at all?","What must happen to the money actually collected as a penalty, and why can't the bank simply keep it?"],"answer":"The bank may impose a penalty clause on this wilful defaulter, since this is a debt (Dayn) arising from a sale (Murabaha), not a pure interest-free loan (Qard). However, the bank cannot keep the penalty as its own income — the proceeds must be given to charity, since a penalty on default cannot become a source of income for the creditor.","explanation":"The book explains that contemporary Shari'ah scholars and bodies (AAOIFI, the OIC Fiqh Council, the Shariat Appellate Bench) permit penalty clauses on financial debt contracts other than pure Qard al-Hasan, specifically to deter wilful default, but is explicit that 'the penalty thus received has to be given to charity' because it cannot become a source of income for the creditor, and that the penalty becomes void if the client proves the default was beyond his control or that the bank suffered no actual loss (7.13).","examTakeaway":"A default penalty on a debt-creating mode is permitted as a deterrent against wilful default, but it must always be routed to charity — never booked as bank profit — which is exactly what keeps it from becoming Riba in another guise.","source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.13","pages":[165,166]},"disclaimer":"Educational scenario for applying Muhammad Ayub's textbook framework — not a Shari'ah ruling or professional financial/legal advice.","productIds":[],"origin":["f2"]},{"id":"cs-working-capital-1","kind":"scenario","title":"Financing More Than Just the Raw Cotton","chapter":14,"topicId":"t14.4.1","conceptLabel":"Mode Selection for Working Capital Financing","modeIds":[],"scenario":"A textile exporter needs financing not only to buy raw cotton, but also to cover the labour and overhead costs of actually manufacturing that cotton into finished, exportable cloth before shipment. The bank's standard Murabaha facility only covers purchasing the raw material.","problem":"Why is a plain Murabaha facility insufficient here, and what combination of modes should the bank structure instead?","prompts":["What kind of cost does Murabaha alone fail to finance in a manufacturing/export business?","Which mode is designed for financing something that still needs to be manufactured, and how can an agency arrangement complete the picture?"],"answer":"Murabaha alone cannot fulfil all of the exporter's requirements, since it only finances the purchase of the raw material, not the processing, labour and overhead needed to turn it into finished goods. The bank should combine Murabaha (for the raw cotton purchase), Istisna'a (to finance manufacture of the finished cloth and pay overhead expenses), and Wakalah (appointing the exporter as its agent to export the goods, with proceeds remitted to the bank, which deducts its Istisna'a cost and profit before the client pays the Murabaha price on schedule).","explanation":"The book states directly that 'Murabaha alone cannot fulfil all the requirements of business and industry' for customers like exporters who also need financing for processing raw material and meeting labour and overhead expenses, and lays out the combined Murabaha + Istisna'a + Wakalah procedure as the bank's product-development solution for working capital in this situation (14.4.1).","examTakeaway":"When a working-capital need spans both buying an input and manufacturing it into a finished product, a single mode like Murabaha is not enough — the bank's own product-development framework combines Murabaha, Istisna'a and Wakalah to cover the whole cycle.","source":{"chapter":14,"section":"14.4.1","pages":[369,370]},"disclaimer":"Educational scenario for applying Muhammad Ayub's textbook framework — not a Shari'ah ruling or professional financial/legal advice.","productIds":[],"origin":["f2"]},{"id":"cs-project-financing-1","kind":"scenario","title":"Why a Lease Won't Build the Cement Plant","chapter":14,"topicId":"t14.4.3","conceptLabel":"Mode Selection for Project Financing","modeIds":[],"scenario":"An industrial group wants its Islamic bank to finance the construction of a brand-new cement plant from the ground up. It initially asks for an Ijarah (leasing) facility, since the bank has used Ijarah for other equipment financing before. The bank's Shari'ah advisor says Ijarah will not work for this particular request.","problem":"Why can't Ijarah finance the construction of a plant that does not yet exist, and what combination of modes should the bank use instead?","prompts":["What does Ijarah require to already exist before it can be used at all?","Which mode is specifically designed to finance something being built or manufactured to order, and how can it be paired with a partnership structure for a large project like this?"],"answer":"Ijarah presupposes an existing asset whose usufruct can be leased — it cannot itself finance the construction of a plant that has not yet been built. Instead, the bank can finance this through a Musharakah with the client to jointly own the completed plant, combined with an Istisna'a contract for its construction/erection (with the client appointed as the bank's agent to supervise the erection); once operational, the bank earns a periodic profit/rental on its ownership share, and recovers its principal as the client progressively purchases the bank's ownership units.","explanation":"The book notes that although Ijarah is currently a popular mode for project finance, 'if the project needs to be installed or constructed, Ijarah cannot be used effectively,' and gives exactly this Musharakah-plus-Istisna'a structure — with the client as supervising agent and unit purchases redeeming the bank's investment — as the alternative for a project like a cement plant's construction/erection (14.4.3).","examTakeaway":"Ijarah requires an already-existing asset; financing something that must first be built calls for Istisna'a (construction) typically paired with Musharakah (joint ownership and profit-sharing) rather than a straight lease.","source":{"chapter":14,"section":"14.4.3","pages":[373,374]},"disclaimer":"Educational scenario for applying Muhammad Ayub's textbook framework — not a Shari'ah ruling or professional financial/legal advice.","productIds":[],"origin":["f2"]},{"id":"cs-appraisal-benchmark-1","kind":"scenario","title":"'Your Profit Rate Is Just Interest by Another Name'","chapter":17,"topicId":"t17.4.3","conceptLabel":"Appraisal: Use of Interest-Based Benchmarks","modeIds":[],"scenario":"A financial journalist writes an op-ed arguing that Islamic bank Murabaha financing is 'interest in disguise,' pointing out that the bank's profit rate is pegged to KIBOR, an interbank interest-rate benchmark, and ends up numerically identical to what a conventional bank would charge on a comparable loan.","problem":"Using the book's own reasoning, how should a knowledgeable MBA respond to this specific objection?","prompts":["Why does the book say Islamic banks currently have little choice but to reference a conventional interest-based benchmark?","Does using that benchmark as a pricing tool mean the underlying transaction is the same as a conventional interest-bearing loan?"],"answer":"Referencing a conventional interest-based benchmark for pricing is not itself Riba: it is used only as a market reference tool for setting the price of goods or usufruct in what remains a trade or lease contract, not as a charge for the time value of money on a debt. The book acknowledges Islamic banks are currently obliged to use conventional benchmarks (because their own market share and dedicated benchmarks are still limited), but stresses this does not make the transaction equivalent to interest — since once the Murabaha price is fixed, it cannot be increased for delay in payment, unlike compounding interest.","explanation":"The book directly rebuts this objection, explaining that IFIs need reference rates like any business needs benchmarks, that they are 'obliged to use the benchmark of the formal conventional market in which they are operating' for now pending development of their own, and that 'the use of a conventional benchmark does not mean that Islamic and conventional banking are similar,' since the underlying subject matter (goods, not money) and the fixity/non-increase of the agreed price make the substance different (17.4.3).","examTakeaway":"Using an interest-rate benchmark for pricing is a pragmatic, temporary market-reference tool for trade/lease contracts, not proof that the transaction itself is interest-based — the test is whether money or debt itself is being priced, not which number is used as a reference point.","source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.4.3","pages":[447,448]},"disclaimer":"Educational scenario for applying Muhammad Ayub's textbook framework — not a Shari'ah ruling or professional financial/legal advice.","productIds":[],"origin":["f2"]},{"id":"cs-appraisal-real-sector-1","kind":"scenario","title":"'You Don't Even Keep an Inventory of Steel'","chapter":17,"topicId":"t17.4.3","conceptLabel":"Appraisal: Actual Involvement in Real Sector Business","modeIds":[],"scenario":"A skeptical corporate client tells his Islamic bank's relationship manager: 'You don't even keep a warehouse of steel — you just do paperwork to facilitate my purchase, exactly like my conventional bank does. There's no real difference between you.'","problem":"Using the book's own rebuttal, what is the substantive difference the bank should point to, given that it agrees it does not maintain inventory?","prompts":["Does the book say holding physical inventory is a Shari'ah requirement for a valid trade transaction?","What does the bank actually have to take on, even briefly, that a conventional bank facilitating the same purchase never takes on?"],"answer":"Not holding inventory does not make the transaction non-compliant — the book explicitly says Islamic banks 'are not grocery stores' and that placing wares on a counter is not a Shari'ah requirement. The substantive difference is that the Islamic bank must take actual ownership and possession of the goods (even via an agent) and bear the ownership/market risk, however briefly, before reselling — whereas in conventional finance, both counter-values can be deferred and rights can be sold onward without the financier ever taking ownership or risk of the underlying asset.","explanation":"The book states that Islamic banks 'have to be involved in real business, with all its implications,' because in Islamic finance 'only one of the items of an exchange contract can be delayed while delivery/possession of the item of exchange has to be given and taken... along with transfer of the risk to the buyer' — unlike conventional finance, where both items can be deferred and even resold without ownership ever transferring — and it explicitly rejects inventory-holding as the relevant test (17.4.3).","examTakeaway":"The book's line between Islamic and conventional financing is not whether the bank stocks physical goods, but whether it genuinely takes on ownership and risk of the asset, even momentarily, before earning a return from reselling or leasing it.","source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.4.3","pages":[450,451]},"disclaimer":"Educational scenario for applying Muhammad Ayub's textbook framework — not a Shari'ah ruling or professional financial/legal advice.","productIds":[],"origin":["f2"]},{"id":"cs-risk-sharing-1","kind":"scenario","title":"A Fixed-Rate Loan or a Profit-Sharing Partner?","chapter":1,"topicId":"t1.2","conceptLabel":"\"No Risk, No Gain\": Risk-Sharing vs Debt-Based Financing","modeIds":[],"scenario":"A small manufacturer needs $50,000 to expand. A conventional bank offers a loan at a fixed interest rate, repayable in full regardless of whether the expansion succeeds. Separately, an Islamic financier offers to provide the same amount as a partner, whose return depends entirely on the venture's actual profit or loss.","problem":"Using the book's own critique of the interest-based system, why is the conventional loan structurally more exploitative than the risk-sharing alternative, and what would the Islamic financier be entitled to (and liable for) instead?","prompts":["What happens to the conventional lender's claim if the manufacturer's expansion fails?","What does \"no risk, no gain\" mean for what the Islamic financier can legitimately earn?"],"answer":"The conventional loan obliges the manufacturer to repay principal plus interest regardless of the business outcome, transferring all risk to the borrower while guaranteeing the lender a risk-free return — exactly the pattern the book blames for unrepayable debt and exploitation. The Islamic partner's entitlement to any profit is conditional on the venture actually succeeding, and the financier must also bear a share of any loss if it fails: profit tied to real risk and responsibility, not a guaranteed charge.","explanation":"The book describes the interest-based system as \"a recipe for exploitation\" that creates unrepayable debt (1.2), states that the prohibition of interest essentially implies \"there can be no gain without risk-sharing\" and that \"no risk, no gain\" is the basic juristic principle of Shari'ah and a normative rule of justice (1.5), and that any entitlement to profit comes from value addition and bearing business risk (1.8).","examTakeaway":"The book's core objection to conventional debt is not the interest rate itself but that it detaches the financier's guaranteed return from the business's actual risk — Islamic finance instead ties any return strictly to risk genuinely borne.","source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.2, 1.5","pages":[5,9]},"disclaimer":"Educational scenario for applying Muhammad Ayub's textbook framework — not a Shari'ah ruling or professional financial/legal advice.","productIds":[],"origin":["f2"]},{"id":"cs-conventional-debt-1","kind":"scenario","title":"Rolling Over a Country's Debt With More Debt","chapter":1,"topicId":"t1.2","conceptLabel":"Interest-Based Debt Accumulation vs Risk-Related Capital","modeIds":[],"scenario":"A developing country facing a budget shortfall is weighing two options: taking on more foreign interest-bearing loans, to be serviced by raising taxes on citizens regardless of whether the funded projects succeed, continuing a pattern of yesterday's debt repaid by today's fresh debt; or attracting foreign direct and portfolio investment in specific real projects, where the investor's return depends on those projects' actual performance.","problem":"Which option does the book warn against as part of a global \"recipe for exploitation,\" and why does it argue that replacing risk-free borrowed capital with risk-related investment breaks the cycle?","prompts":["What does the book say happens when governments raise taxes to service debt without providing citizens any corresponding benefit?","Why does the book prescribe risk-related capital, rather than further borrowing, as the remedy?"],"answer":"The book warns against continuing to borrow on interest to service existing debt — precisely the pattern it identifies as fueling unrepayable debt, forcing governments to raise taxes and charges on the public without providing corresponding services, and worsening inequality. Its prescribed alternative is replacing risk-free debt capital with risk-related, asset/business-based investment (direct and portfolio investment), so the funder's return is tied to real economic performance rather than a guaranteed claim serviced through taxation.","explanation":"The book states that \"yesterday's debt can be repaid by taking out more debt today\" and that governments raise taxes without providing socio-economic amenities to service it (1.2), and that \"the solution lies in replacing risk-free with risk-related capital and making efforts to ensure inflow of foreign resources in the form of direct and portfolio investments\" (1.3).","examTakeaway":"The book's prescribed remedy for the developing-world debt trap is not debt relief alone but a structural shift from risk-free borrowed capital to risk-related direct and portfolio investment.","source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.2, 1.3","pages":[5,8]},"disclaimer":"Educational scenario for applying Muhammad Ayub's textbook framework — not a Shari'ah ruling or professional financial/legal advice.","productIds":[],"origin":["f2"]},{"id":"cs-maqasid-1","kind":"scenario","title":"Seizing a Landowner's Property to Fight Inequality","chapter":2,"topicId":"t2.2.2","conceptLabel":"Maqasid al-Shari'ah: Protection of Property vs Redistributive Justice","modeIds":[],"scenario":"Aiming to reduce inequality, a government plans to seize a portion of a large landowner's property without compensation and outside any of the recognized means of transfer (sale, inheritance, gift, or lawful State grant), redistributing it directly to landless farmers.","problem":"Does Maqasid al-Shari'ah's objective of socio-economic justice justify this seizure, or does another primary objective of Shari'ah constrain the government's options here?","prompts":["Which of Shari'ah's primary objectives specifically protects property, and where does it rank alongside the others?","What legitimate means does the book identify for acquiring or transferring property, and what kind of limit does Islam place on wealth-holding?"],"answer":"No — while establishing justice and equity is a recognized secondary objective of Shari'ah, and reducing the gap between rich and poor is a genuine concern of the Islamic economic system, the protection and preservation of property (alongside religion, life, progeny, intellect and honour) is one of Shari'ah's primary objectives. The book identifies specific legitimate means of acquiring or transferring property — work, inheritance, purchase, gift, and the State granting possession — and notes that Islam's limits on wealth are in terms of the quality/means of acquisition, not an arbitrary cap on quantity. Seizing land outside these recognized means would violate the primary objective of protecting property, even if the stated goal is equity.","explanation":"The book lists \"protection of wealth and property,\" with emphasis on valid (Halal) earning, among Shari'ah's primary objectives (2.2.2), and states that Islam's means of possessing goods are work, inheritance, purchase, gifts and State-granted possession, with limits on acquisition \"in terms of the quality or the means of acquiring and not in terms of quantity of wealth\" (2.5.1).","examTakeaway":"Maqasid al-Shari'ah's pursuit of socio-economic justice operates within, not against, the primary objective of protecting property — Islam regulates the legitimate means of acquiring and disposing of wealth rather than authorizing the State to seize it outside those means.","source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.2.2, 2.5.1","pages":[23,33]},"disclaimer":"Educational scenario for applying Muhammad Ayub's textbook framework — not a Shari'ah ruling or professional financial/legal advice.","productIds":[],"origin":["f2"]},{"id":"cs-welfare-state-1","kind":"scenario","title":"A Universal Cash Handout as the Whole Welfare Policy","chapter":2,"topicId":"t2.5.2","conceptLabel":"Islamic Welfare Approach: Need Fulfilment and the Order of Responsibility","modeIds":[],"scenario":"A government proposes to satisfy its welfare obligations entirely through an equal, unconditional cash transfer to every citizen, regardless of whether they are capable of working, arguing this alone fulfils Islam's welfare mandate.","problem":"Does this equal-cash-transfer approach match how the book frames the Islamic welfare and need-fulfilment obligation, or is a different structure required?","prompts":["What does the book say is the primary way individuals are expected to meet their basic needs, before any State support is triggered?","What order of responsibility does the book lay out for someone who genuinely cannot earn?"],"answer":"No — the book's welfare approach obliges every capable person to work and earn to meet his or her own and dependents' needs first, with the State responsible only for enabling those willing and able to work to find employment. Only those genuinely unable to earn fall to the support of relatives and neighbours and, failing that, the State. An equal cash transfer to everyone regardless of capacity to work bypasses this structured order of responsibility and the emphasis on productive self-sufficiency.","explanation":"The book states that the Islamic economy \"achieves this objective by obliging each capable person to work, enabling him to fulfil his and his dependents' basic needs,\" with the State responsible for enabling the willing and able to find employment, and that only if someone is unable to earn do fellow beings, and ultimately the State, become obliged to support them (2.5.2).","examTakeaway":"Islam's welfare mandate is not a blanket case for indiscriminate State handouts — it follows a structured order (self-effort, then family/community, then the State) targeted at genuine incapacity, in service of Shari'ah's secondary objective of social security.","source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.5.2","pages":[34,35]},"disclaimer":"Educational scenario for applying Muhammad Ayub's textbook framework — not a Shari'ah ruling or professional financial/legal advice.","productIds":[],"origin":["f2"]},{"id":"cs-valid-gains-1","kind":"scenario","title":"A \"Trade Deposit\" That Guarantees Its Return","chapter":4,"topicId":"t4.2.5","conceptLabel":"Valid Gains on Investment (4.2.5)","modeIds":[],"scenario":"An Islamic bank wants to launch a \"Trade Deposit\" marketed as Shari'ah-compliant: depositors' funds are nominally invested in a pool of Murabaha trades, but the bank guarantees depositors a fixed 8% annual return regardless of whether the underlying trades are actually profitable, with depositors bearing no exposure whatsoever to any trade loss.","problem":"Does this product satisfy the book's conditions for \"valid gains on investment,\" or does it simply recreate interest under a trade label?","prompts":["What must a gain be tied to for it to count as legitimate profit rather than Riba?","What happens to the capital owner's claim to profit if he is not exposed to any possibility of loss?"],"answer":"This product fails the test. Genuine investment gain requires the capital to be exposed to direct or indirect business risk — \"along with the entitlement to profit, the liability of risk of loss rests with the capital itself; no other factor can be made to bear the burden of the loss on capital.\" A blanket guarantee of a fixed return regardless of the underlying trades' actual performance decouples the depositors' return from real business risk, which the book's framework does not recognize as valid investment gain.","explanation":"The book states that profit is recognized as a \"reward\" of capital only where the liability of risk of loss also rests with that capital, and that one can earn profit on investment or financing only when it is \"related to certain assets exposed to direct or indirect business risk\" (4.2.5).","examTakeaway":"A fixed return can be legitimate in genuine trade or Ijarah-based products, but only where real asset/business risk is actually passed through to the fund — a flat guarantee regardless of underlying performance is not valid investment gain, whatever label is used.","source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.2.5","pages":[78,79]},"disclaimer":"Educational scenario for applying Muhammad Ayub's textbook framework — not a Shari'ah ruling or professional financial/legal advice.","productIds":[],"origin":["f2"]},{"id":"cs-risk-responsibility-1","kind":"scenario","title":"Locking In Profit While Pushing Off the Risk","chapter":4,"topicId":"t4.2.6","conceptLabel":"Al Kharaj bi-al-Daman: Entitlement to Profit With Risk and Responsibility (4.2.6)","modeIds":[],"scenario":"An Islamic bank has purchased goods for a client under a Murabaha to Purchase Orderer arrangement. Before executing the actual Murabaha sale, it wants the client to bear the risk of the goods (e.g. damage in transit) even though the bank still legally owns them, so the bank can lock in its profit margin without exposure.","problem":"Is this arrangement consistent with the \"Al Kharaj bi-al-Daman\" maxim?","prompts":["Who must bear ownership-related risk while the goods remain in the bank's ownership, under this maxim?","What happens to the bank's claim to profit if it separates ownership from risk this way?"],"answer":"No — under Al Kharaj bi-al-Daman, the bank, as owner, must bear the risk of the goods for as long as it holds them, until the sale is validly executed and risk transfers together with ownership to the buyer. It cannot shift that risk to the client in advance while still profiting from the eventual sale margin; that would mean earning profit while offloading the corresponding liability, which the book identifies as an impermissible transfer of commercial risk without transfer of the related reward.","explanation":"The book states that \"the assumption of business risk is a precondition for entitlement to any profit over the principal,\" that Al Kharaj bi-al-Daman means \"one has to bear loss, if any, if he wants to get any profit over his investment,\" that \"transfer of commercial risk to anyone else without transferring the related reward is not permissible,\" and that \"ownership cannot be separated from the risk of related loss\" (4.2.6).","examTakeaway":"Whoever holds ownership of an asset holds its risk — a bank cannot keep its profit margin while pushing pre-sale ownership risk onto the client; risk and reward of ownership must travel together.","source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.2.6","pages":[81,82]},"disclaimer":"Educational scenario for applying Muhammad Ayub's textbook framework — not a Shari'ah ruling or professional financial/legal advice.","productIds":[],"origin":["f2"]},{"id":"cs-contract-validity-1","kind":"scenario","title":"A Murabaha Price That Depends on When You Pay","chapter":5,"topicId":"t5.7.2","conceptLabel":"Voidable (Fasid) Contracts: Two Sales in One","modeIds":["murabaha"],"scenario":"A bank tells a Murabaha client: \"the price will be $100 if you pay within a month, or $105 if you pay within two months,\" and executes the sale on this basis without the client choosing and fixing one final price at the time of contract.","problem":"What is this contract's status — valid, Fasid (voidable), or Batil (void) — and what, if anything, would fix it?","prompts":["Why does a contract need one single, definite price stipulated at execution?","What is the difference between a defect in a contract's essential elements ('Asl) and a defect in its external attributes (Wasf)?"],"answer":"This is a Fasid (voidable) contract — an example of \"two sales in one,\" listed among the Hanafi categories of voidable contracts, where a single contract relates to two prices without either being finally specified. It is legal in its essential elements ('Asl) but defective in an external attribute (Wasf) — the price ambiguity — so it is capable of correction: once the parties agree on one single, final price before separating, the defect is removed and the contract becomes valid.","explanation":"The book gives almost this exact example — \"If you pay within a month the price will be $100, and if within two months you will be charged $105... the transaction is not valid\" (5.4.2) — and lists \"two sales in one... selling one commodity for two prices, one being cash and the other a credit price... without specifying either\" among the \"Some Forms of Voidable Contracts\" under Hanafi law, noting that a Fasid contract \"can be regularized or validated by removing the cause of irregularity\" (5.7.2).","examTakeaway":"An ambiguous or dual price in a Murabaha is not automatically fatal — under the Hanafi Fasid category, fixing a single stipulated price before the parties separate cures the contract, unlike a defect that strikes at the contract's essential elements.","source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.7.2","pages":[108,122]},"disclaimer":"Educational scenario for applying Muhammad Ayub's textbook framework — not a Shari'ah ruling or professional financial/legal advice.","productIds":[],"origin":["f2"]},{"id":"cs-contract-validity-2","kind":"scenario","title":"Selling Wheat You Don't Own and Hope to Buy Later","chapter":5,"topicId":"t5.5.5","conceptLabel":"Void (Batil) Contracts: Short-Selling of an Unpossessed Commodity","modeIds":[],"scenario":"A commodities trader signs a contract to sell 100 tons of wheat for deferred delivery in three months. He does not own or possess a single ton at the time of contract, takes no advance payment, and simply plans to buy the wheat from the market closer to the delivery date, hoping prices fall in the meantime.","problem":"Since this is neither a Salam nor an Istisna'a arrangement, what is the contract's status, and why?","prompts":["What must be true of the seller's ownership and possession at the time of a valid sale?","Why do Salam and Istisna'a get to be exceptions to that rule when this contract does not?"],"answer":"This is a void (Batil) contract — a straightforward case of short-selling, which the book says has been \"prohibited by almost all scholars,\" because the seller neither owns nor possesses the subject matter at the time of contract and the sale fails the basic conditions of existence, ownership and capacity to deliver, without qualifying for either of the two recognized exceptions (Salam or Istisna'a) that permit selling a not-yet-possessed or nonexistent item under strict conditions.","explanation":"The book states \"if a nonexistent thing is sold, even with mutual consent, the sale is void according to the Shari'ah... Accordingly, short-selling has been prohibited by almost all scholars\" (5.4.2), that valid sales require the commodity to exist and be owned/possessed by the seller, with \"Salam... and Istisna'a\" as \"the only two exceptions to this principle\" (5.5.2), and that a Batil contract, where possession and deliverability are not fulfilled, \"does not give rise to any effect\" (5.7.3).","examTakeaway":"Unlike a Fasid contract's fixable external defect, a Batil contract — such as short-selling an unowned, unpossessed commodity outside the Salam/Istisna'a exceptions — fails on an essential element and produces no legal effect at all; it cannot simply be cured.","source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.5.2, 5.7.3","pages":[110,123]},"disclaimer":"Educational scenario for applying Muhammad Ayub's textbook framework — not a Shari'ah ruling or professional financial/legal advice.","productIds":[],"origin":["f2"]},{"id":"cs-qard-vs-pls-1","kind":"scenario","title":"A Retiree and Her Son Want Very Different Deposits","chapter":8,"topicId":"t8.5.2","conceptLabel":"Current Account (Qard) vs PLS Investment Deposit","modeIds":["qard-hasan"],"scenario":"A retiree wants to park her savings with an Islamic bank; she must be able to withdraw at any time with zero risk of principal loss and doesn't need any return, just safety. Her son, a young professional, wants to deposit his savings for a year, is comfortable with some risk, and wants a shot at a higher return.","problem":"Which deposit structure should the bank recommend to each of them, and why do the two differ so much in risk and return?","prompts":["What contractual basis governs a current account, and what does the bank owe the depositor at all times?","What is different about a PLS investment/savings deposit, and what must be disclosed to the depositor upfront?"],"answer":"The retiree should be offered a current account, based on Qard/Amanah: the bank guarantees her principal as a virtually risk-free loan, but it carries no return (or at most a discretionary, non-obligatory gratis payment), since a Qard cannot legally carry any promised increase. Her son fits a savings or investment deposit on a profit-and-loss-sharing (PLS/Mudarabah) basis: his return is not guaranteed and depends on the bank's actual investment results and a pre-agreed profit-sharing ratio (weighted by deposit tenor), and he could in principle share in a loss if the underlying business genuinely loses money without misconduct.","explanation":"The book states that current deposits generally carry no return because they \"take the form of loans given to Islamic banks and the loans cannot carry any return,\" with the bank guaranteeing the principal, while \"all remunerative deposits in Islamic banks... shall be accepted on a profit and loss sharing (PLS) basis,\" with the profit ratio agreed at account opening and losses shared strictly per the capital ratio (8.5.2).","examTakeaway":"A current account (Qard) trades away any return for a full principal guarantee; a PLS investment deposit trades away that guarantee for a share of real profit (and potential loss) — the bank must match the product to each depositor's actual risk appetite, not blend the two.","source":{"chapter":8,"section":"8.5.2","pages":[188,189]},"disclaimer":"Educational scenario for applying Muhammad Ayub's textbook framework — not a Shari'ah ruling or professional financial/legal advice.","productIds":[],"origin":["f2"]},{"id":"cs-liquidity-management-1","kind":"scenario","title":"Covering a Short-Term Cash Shortfall Without Interest","chapter":8,"topicId":"t8.5.3","conceptLabel":"Islamic Interbank Liquidity Management","modeIds":[],"scenario":"An Islamic bank faces a short-term liquidity shortfall and needs to raise cash quickly without resorting to interest-based interbank borrowing.","problem":"What Shari'ah-compliant tools does the book describe for a bank to manage this kind of short-term liquidity gap?","prompts":["What role can Sukuk play in raising liquidity quickly?","What interbank financing structure does the book describe, and how is the 'price' of such a placement determined instead of an interest rate?"],"answer":"The bank can draw on several tools the book describes: direct interbank placement on a Mudarabah or Musharakah basis, where the deficit bank shares a portion of its profits according to a negotiated or centrally-recommended Mudarabah ratio rather than paying interest; selling Sukuk it holds (e.g. Ijarah Sukuk) in the secondary market, or even to the central bank where regulation allows; and, where available, a central-bank lender-of-last-resort facility structured as a short Mudarabah with a profit ratio weighted toward the central bank to discourage overuse.","explanation":"The book states that \"direct placement or acquisition of funds (in the inter-bank funds market) on the basis of Mudarabah and Musharakah is also possible,\" with the deficit bank sharing profit per a Mudarabah ratio; that \"Sukuk are important for liquidity management... To generate liquidity, Sukuk can be sold/purchased in the secondary market\" or to the central bank; and that Islamic banks may use a central bank lender-of-last-resort arrangement \"on a Mudarabah basis... for, say, a three day grace period\" (8.5.3).","examTakeaway":"Islamic banks manage short-term liquidity gaps not through interest-bearing interbank loans but through Mudarabah/Musharakah-based interbank placements and the sale of Sukuk — tools that keep the return tied to profit-sharing or asset ownership rather than a fixed interest charge.","source":{"chapter":8,"section":"8.5.3","pages":[192,193]},"disclaimer":"Educational scenario for applying Muhammad Ayub's textbook framework — not a Shari'ah ruling or professional financial/legal advice.","productIds":[],"origin":["f2"]},{"id":"cs-dual-taxation-1","kind":"scenario","title":"Why an Islamic Mortgage Gets Taxed Twice","chapter":18,"topicId":"t18.3.2","conceptLabel":"Regulatory and Tax Neutrality for Islamic Finance","modeIds":[],"scenario":"A country's tax code charges a transfer/stamp duty every time real property changes hands. Under a Diminishing Musharakah home-financing structure, the bank must first acquire a share of the house and then progressively transfer units of it to the client, triggering multiple rounds of transfer tax — making the Islamic product markedly more expensive than a conventional mortgage, where the property passes once from developer to homebuyer secured only by a lien.","problem":"What underlying policy problem does the book identify here, and what does it call on regulators to do about it?","prompts":["Why do Islamic modes like Murabaha and Diminishing Musharakah inherently involve more taxable asset transfers than a conventional loan?","What specific regulatory fix does the book recommend, and what real-world precedent does it cite?"],"answer":"The book identifies this as a structural regulatory/tax disadvantage: because Islamic modes require the bank to genuinely acquire and then transfer or resell real assets — rather than merely lending against a lien as in a conventional mortgage — tax regimes not designed with this in mind can subject one economically equivalent transaction to multiple taxable transfers, making Islamic finance artificially uncompetitive. It calls on regulators to build \"flexible regulatory and tax frameworks\" that specifically save \"the IFIs and their customers from dual taxation, particularly in respect of mortgage financing and Murabaha and leasing operations,\" citing the UK FSA's stamp-duty relief for Islamic mortgages as a positive precedent.","explanation":"The book states that \"Islamic banks have to purchase assets for onward sale or lease to their clients. As such, the levy of taxation and fees on their purchases leads to an uneven playing field for them compared with their conventional counterparts,\" and lists \"saving the IFIs and their customers from dual taxation, particularly in respect of mortgage financing and Murabaha and leasing operations\" as a needed regulatory step, noting the UK FSA's flexibility on mortgage stamp duty as a model (18.3.2).","examTakeaway":"A major non-Shari'ah obstacle to Islamic finance is tax-code neutrality — because its contracts require real asset transfers that conventional lending avoids, unmodified tax rules can multiply the tax cost of an economically equivalent transaction, which is a policy fix, not a Shari'ah one.","source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3.2","pages":[470,471]},"disclaimer":"Educational scenario for applying Muhammad Ayub's textbook framework — not a Shari'ah ruling or professional financial/legal advice.","productIds":[],"origin":["f2"]},{"id":"cs-liquidated-damages-1","kind":"scenario","title":"How Much Can the Bank Actually Recover From a Defaulter?","chapter":18,"topicId":"t18.3.2","conceptLabel":"Issues in Islamic Finance: Quantifying a Bank's Loss on Default","modeIds":[],"scenario":"An Islamic bank faces a growing number of overdue receivables, some from wilful defaulters and some from clients in genuine hardship. Its Shari'ah board confirms a penalty on wilful default is permissible and must go to charity, but bank management wants a clear, standard way to also recover its own actual financial loss from a defaulter's delay, and is unsure exactly how a court should quantify that loss.","problem":"What does the book identify as the unresolved practical issue here, and what does it point to as a partial solution?","prompts":["Why can't conventional \"opportunity cost\" simply be used to measure the bank's loss?","What alternative basis have some Shari'ah boards used instead, and what should ultimately guide a court's decision?"],"answer":"The book flags this as a genuinely unresolved practical issue, not one of pure Shari'ah interpretation: it is settled that a default penalty is permissible and must go to charity as a deterrent, and that a bank may separately claim its own actual loss through a court or arbitration, but there is no firm, universally accepted basis for courts to quantify that loss — conventional \"opportunity cost\" cannot be the basis. Some Shari'ah boards allow charging defaulters a rate based on income the bank actually earned from a similar portfolio in the same period, but the book says courts should ultimately weigh the client's financial position and behaviour alongside the bank's actual expenses and time spent in litigation when deciding how much, if any, goes to the bank rather than to charity.","explanation":"The book lists \"the award of liquidated damages to the banks in cases of default\" as an unresolved issue, noting that while \"the penalty paid by the defaulting client goes to the charity fund,\" a bank \"may claim their loss through a court or any independent arbitration; but on what firm basis the courts would be deciding is another related issue. Conventional opportunity cost per se should not be the basis...\" and that \"the deciding factor should be the financial position and the behaviour of the client and the expenses and time involved in litigation\" (18.3.2).","examTakeaway":"Even where Shari'ah principle is settled — penalties go to charity, banks may recover genuine loss — the book flags a real operational gap: no firm, agreed method for courts to quantify a bank's \"actual loss\" from default, as one of Islamic finance's practical, not doctrinal, unresolved issues.","source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3.2","pages":[466]},"disclaimer":"Educational scenario for applying Muhammad Ayub's textbook framework — not a Shari'ah ruling or professional financial/legal advice.","productIds":[],"origin":["f2"]}]);
